#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""stage-engines - the §3.3.2/§6.1.3 build-time engine-assembly script (P4.27).

Runs BEFORE `tauri build` and turns the restored, checksum-verified engine-asset cache
(§6.1.3, keyed `<cache_engine>-<cache_version>-<triple>`) into the two physical bundling shapes §3.3.1
`[DECIDED]`:

  * `bundle.externalBin` SIDECARS -> `src-tauri/binaries/<name>-<target-triple>[.exe]`, the
    target-triple-suffixed naming Tauri requires. The suffix is a STAGE-time convention only:
    Tauri strips it when bundling, so the shipped file beside the app exe is the bare
    `ffmpeg`/`ffmpeg.exe` the §3.3.3 `current_exe().parent()` resolver looks for.
  * `bundle.resources` ENGINE TREES -> `src-tauri/resources/<subdir>/` over the CLOSED §3.3.1
    source-key set {libreoffice, image, fonts}. The bundle-internal TARGET paths
    (`engines/libreoffice/`, `engines/image/`, `fonts/`) are Tauri's mapping, not ours, so the
    §3.3.3 `BaseDirectory::Resource` resolve literals are untouched by where we stage from.

Neither destination ships unless `src-tauri/tauri.conf.json` DECLARES it, so since P4.31 the row
set and that config are bound in BOTH directions at package time: a row with no declaration stages
bytes `tauri build` never packages - silently, the app ships without the engine and faults at
runtime - and a declaration with no row names a path nothing writes.

Per-OS layout (§3.4.5): a `-pc-windows-` triple gets the `.exe` suffix; every other triple gets
the executable bit set on the staged sidecar (a dropped +x otherwise surfaces at runtime as a
misleading startup "integrity failed" rather than a packaging error, G37). §3.4.5's macOS `.app`
column is a RECORDED NO-OP for this script: the `.app` is assembled by `tauri build` out of these
same two destinations; the install_name relocation that follows the merge is HERE since P4.30. The
fat-Mach-O merge itself is HERE since P4.29: on a per-arch triple staging places the restored
bytes unchanged, and on `universal-apple-darwin` it reads BOTH slices and `lipo -create`s them
into one fat artifact - which is why `lipo` runs first and the relocation tail second (the merge
re-emits the Mach-O and would clobber a rewrite applied to a slice).

It NEVER touches the network. It reads only the restored cache directory. Its counterpart is
`scripts/fetch-engine-assets` (P4.28), the checksum-verified pinned-URL POPULATE path that runs
only on a cache miss and is the one script at package time permitted to open a socket; the
SHA-pinned `actions/cache` restore wiring around both is P4.28's L(-1) workflow half, and the
SHA-256 verify against `engines.lock` that must precede staging is G37's, run by the workflow
ahead of this script.
The restored cache legitimately carries entries this script does NOT stage - the P4.34 ruling
(2026-07-22) restores the imgworker's per-triple LINK INPUTS (headers + static libs) under the
same key scheme (`<cache_engine>-<cache_version>-<triple>`, defaulted to each lib's own
id/version), and a header can never ship - so an entry no row
claims is reported, never an error.

SCOPE (P4.27 - the generic skeleton). `STAGED_ENGINES` is the declarative per-engine row set and
it is EMPTY here by design: a staging row is inseparable from the version pin and `engines.lock`
row it stages, so each one is authored by the named, scheduled staging box that owns that engine
(P5.1 / P5.3 / P5.5 / P5.8 / P5.11 / P5.13 image stack, P6.1 FFmpeg, P7.1 / P7.2 LibreOffice +
fonts) - and for LibreOffice specifically §3.3.1 additionally `[DEFER]`s the
launcher-as-externalBin vs launcher-in-resources split to that same §6.1 packaging step.
The MECHANISM below is live and exercised by --selftest; with no rows declared a run is a
target-absent no-op that says so - the same skip-with-notice posture `check-build-matrix` (G30)
holds for its sidecar slice assertion until a sidecar is staged. Each excluded piece is its own
box: the in-bundle hash manifest (P4.41), the §6.1.3 build-assertion framework
(P4.51). The macOS dual-arch `lipo -create` merge was on that list until P4.29 landed it here,
the per-OS rpath/install_name relocation until P4.30 did, and the `tauri.conf.json`
externalBin/resources wiring until P4.31 landed its BINDER here - the config itself lives in
`src-tauri/tauri.conf.json`, and what this file carries is the both-directions check that the two
declarations cannot drift apart - a leg below re-reads THIS sentence against this file's own
decision tags on every run, so the next piece that lands cannot leave itself listed as absent.
Its missing-x86_64-slice from-source fallback is `scripts/compile-engine-asset`'s (P4.29.1), one
step UPSTREAM of here: that script cross-builds the absent slice into the cache, while this one
only ever READS the cache - so a one-slice cache stays a refusal naming the fallback rather than
an attempt to fill the gap, and the one socket-opening actor at package time stays exactly one.

[Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.27] --selftest lives IN this file. The repo's only Python-test
runner is `run-gate-selftests` over `scripts/gate-selftests/`, which is L(-1) (G71) and therefore
not the autonomous Build-Loop's to author; shipping build tooling with no committed test at all
would be worse. So the tool carries its own fixture-driven regression suite here (every entry
point takes `--root`/`--cache-root`/`--target` overrides, the same fixture seam every `check-*`
gate script exposes), and the INDEPENDENT armed canary `scripts/gate-selftests/g24-stage-engines.py`
- which must plant its positives from outside this file, so a neutering edit here cannot neuter
its own check - landed 2026-08-31 as the owner-acked L(-1) hand-off. It loads this file with a
bare `SourceFileLoader` + `module_from_spec`, with NO `sys.modules` entry; `@dataclass` cannot
survive that idiom - it looks its own class's module up in `sys.modules` and dies on the `None` -
which is why the two record types below are `NamedTuple`s. The tool has to be importable under
that idiom, not one bent to fit it. (The idiom is per-tool, not a `g24-*` universal: canaries
that DO register the module exist - g24-plan-lint, whose tool legitimately uses `@dataclass`,
and g24-doc-splice - the no-registration form is the NamedTuple-tool discipline this file and
fetch-engine-assets share.)

Usage:
  stage-engines [--target TRIPLE] [--cache-root DIR] [--root DIR] [--dry-run] [-v]
  stage-engines --check [...]      # resolve + validate only, write nothing
  stage-engines --selftest         # the tool's own fixture-driven regression suite

stdlib-only. Exit: 0 = staged (or nothing declared yet); 1 = a staging violation; 2 = a
structural prerequisite is unusable. Stated as a RULE rather than a list of causes: the list was
already stale once (it named only `lipo`, while an absent `install_name_tool`/`patchelf`/`otool`
and a target with no declared relocator both raise structurally too), and `run_tool`'s own message
was rewritten to name `argv[0]` for exactly that reason while this sentence sixty lines up was
left behind.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import contextlib
import io
import json
import os
import platform
import re
import shutil
import stat
import struct
import subprocess
import sys
import tomllib
import tempfile
from enum import Enum
from pathlib import Path
from typing import NamedTuple

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]

# §3.3.2 step 2 / step 3: the two staging destinations, relative to the repo root. Both are
# §0.7/CLAUDE-§1a-mapped, tracked-by-.gitkeep dirs; their staged CONTENT is build output and is
# gitignored (never committed - DEVELOPMENT.md "Bundled conversion engines").
BINARIES_REL = "src-tauri/binaries"
RESOURCES_REL = "src-tauri/resources"
# The restored engine-asset cache (§6.1.3). P4.28 wires the `actions/cache` restore into this
# path; a local build populates it the same way. Gitignored, like `.gate-tools/`.
DEFAULT_CACHE_REL = ".engine-cache"

# The §3.7.2 manifest the staging rows are bound against (the (A′) binder).
LOCK_REL = "src-tauri/engines.lock"

# The §3.3.1 bundling declaration the staging rows are bound against (the P4.31 binder). Its
# `externalBin`/`resources` paths are relative to the config's OWN directory, so the two prefixes
# are DERIVED from the staging destinations above rather than restated: re-homing where this script
# writes would otherwise leave the config side pointing at the old tree, which is the exact
# both-sides-must-move failure the binder exists to catch. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.31]
TAURI_CONF_REL = "src-tauri/tauri.conf.json"
_CONF_DIR = TAURI_CONF_REL.rsplit("/", 1)[0] + "/"
BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX = BINARIES_REL.removeprefix(_CONF_DIR) + "/"
BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX = RESOURCES_REL.removeprefix(_CONF_DIR) + "/"

# The declared escape hatch to the binder's reverse direction. §3.3.1 lists SIX `externalBin`
# sidecars; five are third-party engines staged out of the §6.1.3 cache, and `convertia-imgworker`
# is not one of them - it is the first-party crate `crates/imgworker` (§3.5.5 / §0.7), compiled by
# cargo and placed beside the others by the build that produced it (P4.34). It can never carry a
# staging row: a row binds to an `engines.lock` entry (`assert_rows_bound_to_manifest`), and that
# manifest is the THIRD-PARTY inventory - every row carries an `upstream_url` and a `source_ref`
# naming an upstream tag/commit, which a first-party build product has none of. Without this,
# P4.34 would meet a landed guard whose only exits are a fake manifest row or weakening the guard.
# NAMED rather than pattern-matched, so a real typo cannot hide behind the exemption; and a name
# that appears here AND in a staging row is itself reported, since the exemption exists only for
# sidecars this script does not produce.
# What still catches an exempt sidecar that was never actually built: `tauri-build`'s own
# `copy_binaries` fails the COMPILE with "<path> does not exist". That is the later, file-shaped
# message this binder's reverse direction exists to pre-empt - so the exemption is a deliberate
# trade of the earlier diagnostic for a check that can be satisfied at all, not a hole.
# [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.31]
BUILT_SIDECARS = ("convertia-imgworker",)

# §3.3.1's `bundle.resources` map fixes the SOURCE keys, so this set is closed: a resource row
# naming anything else would stage a tree Tauri never bundles. P5-P7 fill rows INTO these three,
# they do not add a fourth (that would be a §3.3.1 change, i.e. a spec edit first).
RESOURCE_SUBDIRS = ("libreoffice", "image", "fonts")

# §3.4.5's v1 target triples. The `[DEFER]`red Windows-aarch64 and Linux-musl triples are absent
# on purpose: an unknown triple must fail clearly rather than stage under a name Tauri will not
# resolve. `universal-apple-darwin` is deliberately NOT here - see resolve_target().
V1_TARGET_TRIPLES = (
    "x86_64-pc-windows-msvc",
    "aarch64-apple-darwin",
    "x86_64-apple-darwin",
    "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu",
)
UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE = "universal-apple-darwin"
# The two per-arch triples a universal artifact is merged FROM. Ordered arm64-first because the
# universal build runs on an arm64 runner (§6.1.3), so the native slice is the one a cache miss is
# least likely to hit - the x86_64 slice is the documented hard case P4.29.1 falls back for.
UNIVERSAL_SLICES = ("aarch64-apple-darwin", "x86_64-apple-darwin")

# Mach-O FAT header, big-endian. Parsed here rather than shelled out to `lipo -info` for the same
# reason `scripts/check-build-matrix` parses it (P0.4.10): `lipo` is macOS-only, while this
# assertion has to be drivable from the suite on any runner. The MERGE genuinely needs `lipo` and
# only ever runs on macOS; the CHECK does not, and a check that cannot run is not a check.
# [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29]
_FAT_MAGIC = 0xCAFEBABE
_FAT_MAGIC_64 = 0xCAFEBABF
_CPU_TYPE_X86_64 = 0x01000007
_CPU_TYPE_ARM64 = 0x0100000C
_CPU_TYPE_NAMES = {_CPU_TYPE_X86_64: "x86_64", _CPU_TYPE_ARM64: "arm64"}
# A non-Mach-O whose first four bytes happen to be `0xCAFEBABE` - a Java `.class` is the classic -
# would otherwise be read as a fat binary with an absurd arch count. Bounded, fail-closed.
# The value is the GATE's (`scripts/check-build-matrix`, P0.4.10), deliberately: producer and gate
# answer the same question about the same bytes, and two different bounds would mean one of them
# accepts a file the other refuses. A freeze leg pins the pair. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29]
_MAX_FAT_ARCH = 64
# Every fat-header constant above, mapped to its name in the G30 gate. A TABLE rather than one
# pinned value per leg: pinning them one at a time is exactly how the next constant arrives
# unpinned - the self-test asserts both that each pair agrees AND that this mapping covers every
# such constant this module defines. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29]
_GATE_PINNED_CONSTANTS = {
    "FAT_MAGIC": "_FAT_MAGIC",
    "FAT_MAGIC_64": "_FAT_MAGIC_64",
    "CPU_TYPE_X86_64": "_CPU_TYPE_X86_64",
    "CPU_TYPE_ARM64": "_CPU_TYPE_ARM64",
    "_MAX_FAT_ARCH": "_MAX_FAT_ARCH",
}
# The read size `same_bytes` streams in. A module constant so a fixture can shrink it and drive
# the loop ACROSS a chunk boundary: with a 1 MiB read every realistic test file arrives whole, so
# a comparison that stopped after the first chunk would answer correctly on every fixture.
# Homed BELOW the gate-pinned table, not above `_MAX_FAT_ARCH`: inserted there it sat between that
# constant and the comment block explaining it, which then read as describing this one and was
# false in every clause. G74 catches that splice in `.rs` only, so nothing mechanical sees the
# Python form. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29]
_COMPARE_CHUNK = 1 << 20

# The §3.4.5 "Engine exe extension" row keys off the triple, never off the HOST: a cross-named
# stage (e.g. --target for another platform in a self-test) must still get the right suffix.
_WINDOWS_TRIPLE_MARKER = "-pc-windows-"

# (platform.system(), platform.machine()) -> the §3.4.5 triple for that runner.
_HOST_TRIPLES = {
    ("Windows", "AMD64"): "x86_64-pc-windows-msvc",
    ("Windows", "x86_64"): "x86_64-pc-windows-msvc",
    ("Darwin", "arm64"): "aarch64-apple-darwin",
    ("Darwin", "x86_64"): "x86_64-apple-darwin",
    ("Linux", "x86_64"): "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu",
}


class StagingError(Exception):
    """A staging violation (exit 1) or an unusable prerequisite (exit 2, `structural=True`)."""

    def __init__(self, msg: str, *, structural: bool = False) -> None:
        super().__init__(msg)
        self.structural = structural


class StagingKind(Enum):
    SIDECAR = "sidecar"
    RESOURCE_TREE = "resource-tree"


class EngineStaging(NamedTuple):
    """One declared per-engine staging row (§3.3.2 step 2 / step 3).

    cache_engine: the `<cache_engine>` token of the §6.1.3 cache key
                  `<cache_engine>-<cache_version>-<triple>`. It names the ENTRY, not the artifact:
                  `engines.lock` rows declare the same token when they ship out of one download
                  (the (A′) ruling), which is exactly why `member` below can address `ffprobe`
                  INSIDE the `ffmpeg` entry.
    kind:         which of the two §3.3.1 bundling mechanisms this row uses.
    dest:         SIDECAR -> the BARE binary name (§3.3.3's EngineId->binary-name table, e.g.
                  "ffmpeg"); RESOURCE_TREE -> the RESOURCE_SUBDIRS member to stage into.
    member:       the path INSIDE the restored cache entry to take ("" = the entry root). A
                  sidecar row names the file (one cache entry can carry several, e.g. ffmpeg and
                  ffprobe); a resource row names the subtree, or "" for the whole entry.
    """

    cache_engine: str
    kind: StagingKind
    dest: str
    member: str = ""


# The per-engine rows. EMPTY at P4.27 by design - see the module docstring's SCOPE paragraph: a
# staging row is inseparable from the version pin and `engines.lock` row it stages, so each one is
# authored by the P5-P7 staging box that owns that engine (and for LibreOffice specifically,
# §3.3.1 additionally `[DEFER]`s the launcher-as-externalBin vs launcher-in-resources split to
# that same step). SEVERAL rows may share one RESOURCE_TREE destination - §3.3.1 maps the whole
# image stack onto `resources/image/` - which is what clear_resource_destinations() is keyed on.
STAGED_ENGINES: tuple[EngineStaging, ...] = ()


class StagedResult(NamedTuple):
    row: EngineStaging
    entry: Path | None  # the matched cache entry, None when the engine is not in the cache
    dest: Path | None  # the staged destination, None when nothing was staged


class Relocation(NamedTuple):
    """One staged file's §3.5 beside-the-exe load-path rewrite (P4.30).

    DATA, exactly like `EngineStaging`: the per-engine box that stages a dynamically-linked
    component set declares WHAT must be rewritten, and the per-OS functions below decide HOW on the
    target platform. The mechanism ships here; the first filler is P6.1.2 (FFmpeg + its five §3.1
    row-2a component libs).

    WHY IT IS NEEDED AT ALL. §3.5 clears `LD_PRELOAD`/`LD_LIBRARY_PATH`/`DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES`/
    `DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH` from the engine environment and §3.3.3 resolves programs by absolute
    bundled path rather than `PATH`, so no environment channel is left for the dynamic loader.
    The ONLY way a component lib shipped BESIDE the exe (§3.9.1's v1 preference) resolves is a
    RELATIVE load path baked into the binary - and upstream/distro builds carry ABSOLUTE
    `install_name`/`RUNPATH` entries, so as staged they resolve to paths that do not exist on the
    user's machine.

    Fields:
      dest:       the `EngineStaging.dest` of the row that stages this file - the binding key. A
                  declaration naming no staged row is refused (`assert_relocations_bound`), the
                  same "a line nothing binds to never runs" rule the configure seam holds.
      member:     the path INSIDE that row's staged destination, "" for the row's own file. A
                  SIDECAR row stages one file, so its declarations leave this empty; a
                  RESOURCE_TREE row stages a subtree, so each rewritten file names itself.
      set_id:     macOS only - rewrite this file's OWN install name (`-id`). True for a shipped
                  component LIB, False for the dependent binary that merely references them.
      references: macOS only - the `(absolute install name as built, bundled basename)` pairs to
                  rewrite (`-change`). These are per-BUILD facts that can only be read off the
                  binary's load commands, so the per-engine box declares them rather than this
                  mechanism guessing.
      origin:     where the libs sit relative to the file being rewritten, "." for beside it.
                  Shapes BOTH legs: `@loader_path/<origin>` on macOS, `$ORIGIN/<origin>` on Linux.
    """

    dest: str
    member: str = ""
    set_id: bool = False
    references: tuple[tuple[str, str], ...] = ()
    origin: str = "."


# The per-engine relocation declarations. EMPTY here by design, exactly as `STAGED_ENGINES` is: a
# relocation is inseparable from the component set it rewrites, so it is authored by the box that
# stages that set. P6.1.2 (FFmpeg + libmp3lame/libvorbis/libogg/libopus/libvpx) is the first and,
# per the §3.6.1 model, close to the only one - the image stack is STATICALLY linked into
# `convertia-imgworker` (P4.34) and x265 is a libheif PLUGIN discovered through
# `LIBHEIF_PLUGIN_PATH` (P5.10), so neither is a beside-the-exe lib and neither needs a rewrite.
# That absence is a recorded decision, not an oversight.
RELOCATIONS: tuple[Relocation, ...] = ()


def fail(msg: str) -> None:
    print(f"[stage-engines] {msg}", file=sys.stderr)


def note(msg: str) -> None:
    print(f"[stage-engines] {msg}")


def host_target_triple() -> str | None:
    """The §3.4.5 triple for the current runner, or None if this host has no v1 triple."""
    return _HOST_TRIPLES.get((platform.system(), platform.machine()))


def resolve_target(explicit: str | None) -> str:
    """The triple to stage for: an explicit --target, else the host's. Raises StagingError."""
    if explicit == UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE:
        # Supported since P4.29: both per-arch slices are sourced from the cache and merged with
        # `lipo -create` into one fat Mach-O, because Tauri does NOT lipo sidecars (§6.1.3,
        # `[DECIDED - verified vs tauri#3355]`) - it reads the universal-named file as-is.
        return UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE
    if explicit is not None:
        if explicit not in V1_TARGET_TRIPLES:
            raise StagingError(
                f"unknown target triple '{explicit}'; the §3.4.5 v1 set is "
                f"{', '.join(V1_TARGET_TRIPLES)}",
                structural=True,
            )
        return explicit
    host = host_target_triple()
    if host is None:
        raise StagingError(
            f"no §3.4.5 v1 target triple for this host "
            f"({platform.system()}/{platform.machine()}); pass --target explicitly",
            structural=True,
        )
    return host


def fat_slice_cputypes(path: Path) -> tuple[int, ...]:
    """The CPU types a fat Mach-O declares, or () for anything that is not one.

    Reads the header only - the slices themselves are not parsed, because the question is which
    architectures the file CLAIMS to carry, which is exactly what `lipo -info` reports.
    """
    with path.open("rb") as handle:
        header = handle.read(8)
        if len(header) < 8:
            return ()
        magic, count = struct.unpack(">II", header)
        if magic not in (_FAT_MAGIC, _FAT_MAGIC_64):
            return ()
        if count == 0 or count > _MAX_FAT_ARCH:
            return ()
        width = 32 if magic == _FAT_MAGIC_64 else 20
        table = handle.read(width * count)
    if len(table) < width * count:
        return ()
    return tuple(
        # UNSIGNED, matching the G30 gate's own `">IIIII"` fat_arch read and the sibling parser in
        # `compile-engine-asset`. Signed was not WRONG - Apple's `cpu_type_t` really is a signed
        # int32 - it DISAGREED with the gate that judges these same bytes, and identically for
        # every real cputype (all below 2^31), so no fixture of real values could tell the two
        # readers apart. That is how a latent producer-vs-gate disagreement survives unnoticed;
        # the sibling's cross-check now drives a high-bit cputype so it cannot again.
        # [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29.1]
        struct.unpack_from(">I", table, index * width)[0] for index in range(count)
    )


# The thin Mach-O magics, both byte orders. A universal artifact's INPUTS are thin; its output
# is fat. Anything else in a tree (a font, a config, a data blob) is byte-compared instead.
_MACHO_MAGICS = (
    b"\xcf\xfa\xed\xfe", b"\xce\xfa\xed\xfe",  # MH_MAGIC_64 / MH_MAGIC, little-endian
    b"\xfe\xed\xfa\xcf", b"\xfe\xed\xfa\xce",  # ...big-endian
    b"\xca\xfe\xba\xbe", b"\xca\xfe\xba\xbf",  # already fat
)


def is_macho(path: Path) -> bool:
    """Does this file carry a Mach-O magic? Decides lipo-vs-copy in a resource tree."""
    if not path.is_file():
        return False
    with path.open("rb") as handle:
        return handle.read(4) in _MACHO_MAGICS


def assert_universal_fat(path: Path, label: str) -> None:
    """Refuse a `*-universal-apple-darwin` artifact that is not fat with BOTH slices.

    This is the staging-time half of G30. The gate (`scripts/check-build-matrix`) makes the same
    assertion over the whole staged tree before `tauri build`; making it here too is not
    redundancy for its own sake - it fails at the step that PRODUCED the file, naming the engine,
    instead of at a later sweep that can only name a path. A single-arch binary carrying the
    universal NAME is the silent case G30 exists for: Tauri bundles it without complaint and the
    user on the other architecture gets an illegal-instruction crash.
    """
    cputypes = fat_slice_cputypes(path)
    if not cputypes:
        raise StagingError(
            f"{label}: {path.name} is named for the universal target but is not a fat Mach-O - "
            "Tauri bundles it as-is (it does not lipo sidecars, §6.1.3), so a thin binary here "
            "ships a crash on the other architecture (G30)"
        )
    missing = [
        _CPU_TYPE_NAMES[cpu]
        for cpu in (_CPU_TYPE_ARM64, _CPU_TYPE_X86_64)
        if cpu not in cputypes
    ]
    if missing:
        present = ", ".join(_CPU_TYPE_NAMES.get(c, hex(c)) for c in cputypes) or "none"
        raise StagingError(
            f"{label}: {path.name} is fat but carries only [{present}] - both arm64 AND x86_64 "
            f"are required for a universal artifact, missing [{', '.join(missing)}] (G30)"
        )


def run_tool(argv: list[str]) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess[str]:
    """The real external-tool seam. Replaced wholesale by the fixtures.

    ONE seam for every subprocess this script spawns - `lipo` for the universal merge (P4.29) and
    the per-OS relocation tools for the load-path rewrite (P4.30) - so there is exactly one place a
    process can start and exactly one thing a self-test has to inject. It was `run_lipo` until
    P4.30 gave it a second caller; the body never named the tool, only the docstring did.
    [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]

    `shell=False` and an argv LIST: the paths are cache-entry and staging paths, and joining them
    into a shell line is how a directory with a space becomes two arguments.

    An ABSENT tool is a missing prerequisite, not a staging violation: it surfaces as an `OSError`,
    which `main` reports as exit 1 alongside genuine filesystem faults, and the two read very
    differently to whoever is looking at a red CI leg. Named and re-raised structurally (exit 2),
    the way an absent cache root already is. The message names the tool it could not run rather
    than a fixed list of tools, so a leg that adds one cannot leave this text behind.
    """
    try:
        return subprocess.run(
            argv, capture_output=True, text=True, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace", check=False
        )
    except FileNotFoundError as absent:
        raise StagingError(
            f"`{argv[0]}` could not be run - it is not on PATH. Staging for this target needs it "
            "(§6.1.3), so this target cannot be staged from a machine without it. A "
            f"`{argv[0]}` that IS present and fails reports through its exit code instead. "
            "(Named rather than enumerated: a message listing every tool the seam can carry reads "
            "wrong on the leg that is missing only one of them.)",
            structural=True,
        ) from absent


def same_bytes(a: Path, b: Path) -> bool:
    """Do two files hold the same bytes? Streaming, and with no setting to get wrong.

    NOT `filecmp.cmp`. Two reasons, and the second is why this function exists rather than a
    careful call: it takes a `shallow` argument whose DEFAULT is the dangerous one - `shallow=True`
    answers from `(size, mtime)` alone, so two DIFFERING same-size slices written in one timestamp
    tick compare equal, the caller takes the already-universal copy path, and slice 0 ships
    verbatim under the universal name. That is the half-universal class G30 exists for, reachable
    by editing one keyword. It also caches on `(paths, size, mtime)`, which is safe here only
    because each pair is compared once per run - safe by accident is not a property.

    Streaming for the ordinary reason: this compares the two slices of members like LibreOffice's
    `libmergedlo.dylib` (~150 MB), and reading both into memory to answer one bool holds 300 MB.

    No size short-circuit. It would be a pure optimisation - the loop answers a size mismatch on
    its own, because the shorter side runs out first and yields fewer bytes than the other for
    that read - and an optimisation no leg can distinguish from its absence is a branch carried on
    trust. What it could have saved is small: on the SIDECAR path both operands are Mach-O, so two
    differing slices differ in the header's cputype field inside the first chunk; on the tree path
    an arch-independent member (`shared.rdb` and its kind) can differ arbitrarily late, and there
    the streaming read is what avoids holding both copies at once anyway.
    [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29]
    """
    with a.open("rb") as left, b.open("rb") as right:
        while True:
            chunk_a, chunk_b = left.read(_COMPARE_CHUNK), right.read(_COMPARE_CHUNK)
            if chunk_a != chunk_b:
                return False
            if not chunk_a:
                return True


def merge_universal_binary(
    sources: list[Path], dest: Path, label: str, *, runner, finalize=None
) -> None:
    """Merge per-arch slices into one fat Mach-O at `dest`, or leave `dest` untouched.

    Tauri does NOT lipo sidecars (§6.1.3, `[DECIDED - verified vs tauri#3355]`) - it reads
    `binaries/<name>-universal-apple-darwin` as-is - so this merge is entirely this script's job.

    ATOMIC, in the §2.0 shape the rest of the repo uses: `lipo` writes a sibling temp, the G30
    fatness assertion runs on THAT, and only a passing merge is moved into place. Merging straight
    into `dest` and asserting afterwards left a refused - thin - binary sitting under the universal
    name, where a later run whose engine is simply absent from the cache exits 0 and ships it.

    `finalize` runs on the STAGING file, before the move, and both call sites use it for the same
    thing: the merged file's MODE. `lipo -create` writes its own, so the sidecar has to add the
    exec bit and a tree member has to inherit its slice's - and doing either AFTER the move leaves
    a window, and an interrupted run, in which a fat but non-executable binary already wears the
    final name. The mode is part of what is published, so it is published atomically with the
    bytes. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29]
    """
    dest.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    staging = dest.with_name(f".{dest.name}.merging")
    staging.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    try:
        argv = ["lipo", "-create", *[str(src) for src in sources], "-output", str(staging)]
        result = runner(argv)
        if getattr(result, "returncode", 1) != 0:
            detail = (getattr(result, "stderr", "") or getattr(result, "stdout", "")).strip()
            raise StagingError(f"{label}: `lipo -create` failed:\n{detail}")
        if not staging.is_file():
            raise StagingError(
                f"{label}: `lipo -create` reported success but wrote no {dest.name}"
            )
        assert_universal_fat(staging, label)
        if finalize is not None:
            finalize(staging)
        os.replace(staging, dest)
    finally:
        staging.unlink(missing_ok=True)


def exe_suffix(triple: str) -> str:
    """§3.4.5: `.exe` on Windows, nothing elsewhere - keyed off the TRIPLE, not the host."""
    return ".exe" if _WINDOWS_TRIPLE_MARKER in triple else ""


def sidecar_filename(binary: str, triple: str) -> str:
    """The externalBin stage-time name Tauri requires: `<name>-<target-triple>[.exe]`."""
    return f"{binary}-{triple}{exe_suffix(triple)}"


def sidecar_dest(root: Path, binary: str, triple: str) -> Path:
    return root / BINARIES_REL / sidecar_filename(binary, triple)


def resource_dest(root: Path, subdir: str) -> Path:
    if subdir not in RESOURCE_SUBDIRS:
        raise StagingError(
            f"resource subdir '{subdir}' is not a §3.3.1 `bundle.resources` source key "
            f"({', '.join(RESOURCE_SUBDIRS)}) - Tauri would never bundle it"
        )
    return root / RESOURCES_REL / subdir


def cache_entry_matches(name: str, cache_engine: str, triple: str) -> bool:
    """Does this cache dir name parse as `<cache_engine>-<cache_version>-<triple>`?

    The `<cache_version>` is matched by exclusion rather than by a version grammar: both the engine
    name (`convertia-imgworker`) and a version (`7.1-full_build`) can carry dashes, so anchoring
    on the two ends and requiring a non-empty middle is the only unambiguous parse.
    """
    prefix, suffix = f"{cache_engine}-", f"-{triple}"
    return (
        name.startswith(prefix)
        and name.endswith(suffix)
        and len(name) > len(prefix) + len(suffix)
    )


def select_cache_entry(cache_root: Path, cache_engine: str, triple: str) -> Path | None:
    """The one restored cache entry for (engine, triple), or None. Two = fail-closed.

    Two entries mean two pinned versions of one engine are restored at once; picking either would
    silently decide which version ships, so it is an error, not a heuristic.
    """
    matches = sorted(
        d for d in cache_root.iterdir() if d.is_dir() and cache_entry_matches(d.name, cache_engine, triple)
    )
    if len(matches) > 1:
        raise StagingError(
            f"ambiguous engine-asset cache: {len(matches)} entries for '{cache_engine}' at "
            f"{triple} ({', '.join(m.name for m in matches)}) - exactly one pinned version may "
            "be restored per engine per triple (§6.1.3)"
        )
    return matches[0] if matches else None


def unclaimed_cache_entries(
    cache_root: Path, rows: tuple[EngineStaging, ...], triple: str
) -> list[str]:
    """Cache entries for the RESOLVED triple that no declared row claims - INFORMATIONAL only.

    Deliberately not an error. The P4.34 ruling (2026-07-22) restores the imgworker's per-triple
    LINK INPUTS (headers + static libs for libheif/libde265/librsvg/cgif/libimagequant) from this
    same cache under the same key scheme (defaulted to each lib's own id/version); those are compile inputs that
    must never be staged, so "every restored entry has a staging row" is simply false. Surfacing
    them under -v keeps a genuinely forgotten row visible without inventing a rule the plan
    contradicts. Entries for OTHER triples are none of this run's business and are skipped.
    """
    claimed = {r.cache_engine for r in rows}
    unclaimed = []
    for d in sorted(cache_root.iterdir()):
        if not d.is_dir() or not d.name.endswith(f"-{triple}"):
            continue
        if not any(cache_entry_matches(d.name, engine, triple) for engine in claimed):
            unclaimed.append(d.name)
    return unclaimed


def ambiguous_engine_entries(
    cache_root: Path, rows: tuple[EngineStaging, ...], triple: str
) -> list[tuple[str, list[str]]]:
    """Cache entries that parse as belonging to MORE THAN ONE declared engine - fail-closed.

    `cache_entry_matches` anchors on the two ends and treats the middle as the version, so a
    declared engine whose name is a dash-prefix of another (`libvips` vs a hypothetical
    `libvips-tools`) would claim the longer one's entries as version `tools-<ver>`. Picking either
    reading would silently decide which artifact ships, so an entry two declared engines both
    claim is an error rather than a longest-prefix heuristic.
    """
    engines = sorted({r.cache_engine for r in rows})
    collisions = []
    for d in sorted(cache_root.iterdir()):
        if not d.is_dir():
            continue
        claimants = [e for e in engines if cache_entry_matches(d.name, e, triple)]
        if len(claimants) > 1:
            collisions.append((d.name, claimants))
    return collisions


def manifest_cache_engines(lock_path: Path) -> set[str] | None:
    """The `cache_engine` group tokens §3.7.2's manifest declares, or None when it declares no rows.

    None is the FAIL-OPEN signal, and it is the honest one: `src-tauri/engines.lock` is L(-1)-caged
    and still an empty skeleton, so there is nothing to bind against until the P5-P7 staging boxes
    land their rows. The moment the first row lands the binder below fails CLOSED.

    A deliberately minimal read: `crate::engines::lock` is the authoritative validator and
    `scripts/fetch-engine-assets` the populate-side reader. This one looks at exactly two keys.
    """
    if not lock_path.is_file():
        return None
    try:
        doc = tomllib.loads(lock_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except tomllib.TOMLDecodeError as exc:
        raise StagingError(f"{lock_path}: not valid TOML - {exc}") from exc
    rows = doc.get("engine", [])
    if not isinstance(rows, list):
        # Fail CLOSED on a mis-typed key: "no rows yet" and "the rows are unreadable" are
        # different situations, and only the first one is safe to wave through.
        raise StagingError(f"{lock_path}: `engine` must be an array of tables")
    if not rows:
        return None
    tokens: set[str] = set()
    for index, row in enumerate(rows):
        if not isinstance(row, dict):
            raise StagingError(f"{lock_path}: `engine[{index}]` is not a table")
        declared = row.get("cache_engine")
        if declared is not None and (not isinstance(declared, str) or not declared.strip()):
            raise StagingError(
                f"{lock_path}: `engine[{index}]`: `cache_engine` is declared but unusable"
            )
        token = declared if declared is not None else row.get("id")
        if not isinstance(token, str) or not token.strip():
            raise StagingError(
                f"{lock_path}: `engine[{index}]` names no cache entry - it declares neither"
                f" a usable `cache_engine` nor a usable `id`"
            )
        tokens.add(token.strip())
    # "declares no rows" and "declares rows I cannot read" are different situations, and only
    # the first is safe to wave through: a blanked `id` on the only row would otherwise DISARM
    # the binder, which is the very typo class it exists to catch. The sibling reader
    # (`scripts/fetch-engine-assets.parse_lock`) refuses THESE shapes too; it is additionally
    # stricter on token GRAMMAR, on field PRESENCE (`kind`, `acquisition`, `triples`,
    # `asset_sha256`-iff-prebuilt) and on §3.4.5 TRIPLE MEMBERSHIP - none of which this minimal
    # read models, and all of which can only widen fail-open on a manifest both it and the
    # Rust validator already reject.
    return tokens


def unbound_staging_rows(
    rows: tuple[EngineStaging, ...], lock_path: Path
) -> list[str]:
    """Staging rows whose cache entry NO `engines.lock` group declares.

    The two sides of §6.1.3 are written in different files by different boxes: a P5-P7 staging box
    adds an `EngineStaging` row here, and the owner adds the matching `engines.lock` row as an
    L(-1) act. Nothing mechanical held them together, so a typo in either token would surface as an
    empty cache lookup at package time rather than as a failing check. This is that binder.
    """
    declared = manifest_cache_engines(lock_path)
    if declared is None:
        return []
    return sorted({row.cache_engine for row in rows} - declared)


def _set_exec_bit(path: Path) -> None:
    """§3.4.5: the executable bit must be set on a non-Windows staged sidecar."""
    mode = path.stat().st_mode
    path.chmod(mode | stat.S_IXUSR | stat.S_IXGRP | stat.S_IXOTH)


def _member_path(entry: Path, member: str) -> Path:
    """Resolve a row's `member` INSIDE its cache entry, refusing any escape from it."""
    candidate = (entry / member).resolve() if member else entry.resolve()
    root = entry.resolve()
    if candidate != root and root not in candidate.parents:
        raise StagingError(f"cache member '{member}' escapes its entry {entry.name}")
    return candidate


def stage_sidecar(entry: Path, row: EngineStaging, triple: str, root: Path, *, write: bool) -> Path:
    src = _member_path(entry, row.member or row.dest)
    if not src.is_file():
        raise StagingError(
            f"sidecar '{row.dest}': {src} is missing from the restored cache entry {entry.name}"
        )
    dest = sidecar_dest(root, row.dest, triple)
    if write:
        dest.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        shutil.copy2(src, dest)
        if not exe_suffix(triple):
            _set_exec_bit(dest)
    return dest


def stage_resource_tree(entry: Path, row: EngineStaging, root: Path, *, write: bool) -> Path:
    src = _member_path(entry, row.member)
    if not src.is_dir():
        raise StagingError(
            f"resource tree '{row.dest}': {src} is missing from the restored cache entry "
            f"{entry.name} (a `bundle.resources` row stages a DIRECTORY, §3.3.1)"
        )
    dest = resource_dest(root, row.dest)
    # BEFORE any byte moves, and on --check too: a tree that would carry an escaping link is
    # refused without writing one, so nothing out-of-entry ever lands in src-tauri/.
    assert_no_escaping_links(src, row)
    if write:
        dest.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        # dirs_exist_ok because SEVERAL rows legitimately share one destination: §3.3.1 maps the
        # whole image stack onto the single `resources/image/` source key and the six P5 boxes
        # (P5.1/P5.3/P5.5/P5.8/P5.11/P5.13) each stage their own cache entry into it. The
        # replace-don't-merge clear is therefore per DESTINATION, once per run, in
        # clear_resource_destinations() - doing it here would make row N erase rows 1..N-1.
        # symlinks=True preserves links instead of dereferencing them: a real LibreOffice/.app
        # tree is full of internal symlinks, and dereferencing would let a tampered cache entry
        # (§0.11 T3a, the Actions-cache poisoning class G37 names) pull an out-of-entry file INTO
        # the bundle. Preserving is not itself a refusal, so assert_no_escaping_links above is the
        # actual tree-content counterpart of the _member_path guard.
        shutil.copytree(src, dest, symlinks=True, dirs_exist_ok=True)
    return dest


def clear_resource_destinations(
    root: Path, rows: tuple[EngineStaging, ...], *, write: bool
) -> None:
    """Clear each DISTINCT resource destination once per run, BEFORE any row copies into it.

    Replace, never merge: an `engines.lock` version bump re-staged over the previous tree would
    otherwise leave behind files the new version dropped, and P4.41's hash manifest + G35's SBOM
    are generated FROM what is staged, so nothing downstream would notice. Keyed on the
    DESTINATION rather than on the row, because §3.3.1 maps several engines onto one source key.

    Consequence P5-P7 inherit: a declared row whose cache entry is ABSENT is not an error (§6.1.3
    lets a platform ship without a §3.4-unavailable engine), so a total cache miss now leaves the
    destination DELETED rather than stale. That is the safe direction - an empty destination fails
    P4.42's §7.2.3 presence loop, where a leftover stale tree would have passed it. A row that
    fails mid-loop leaves a partially-cleared destination, which is harmless: the run exits
    non-zero so `tauri build` never consumes it, the next run clears and repopulates, and these
    are gitignored build outputs, never user files (§2.0 never-harm is about the SOURCE set).
    """
    if not write:
        return
    for subdir in sorted({r.dest for r in rows if r.kind is StagingKind.RESOURCE_TREE}):
        dest = resource_dest(root, subdir)
        if dest.exists():
            shutil.rmtree(dest)


def _link_target(path: Path) -> Path:
    r"""Where a symlink or junction points: absolute, prefix-normalised, never raising.

    `os.path.realpath`, NOT `os.readlink` + `Path.resolve()`: on Windows `os.readlink` SUCCEEDS on
    a junction but returns a `\\?\`-prefixed path that `Path.resolve()` does not normalise, so
    containment could never hold and every junction - internal ones included - was refused with a
    wrong diagnosis. `realpath` strips the prefix, resolves both link shapes, and returns a
    lexically-resolved path for a dangling target instead of raising.
    """
    return Path(os.path.realpath(path))


def assert_no_escaping_links(src: Path, row: EngineStaging) -> None:
    """Refuse a SOURCE tree carrying a symlink or junction that resolves OUTSIDE that tree.

    The tree-content counterpart of _member_path, and it runs on the SOURCE, before the copy, for
    a reason the destination cannot give: `shutil.copytree` keys on `is_symlink()`, which is FALSE
    for a **Windows directory junction**, so it recurses INTO a junction and MATERIALISES the
    out-of-entry content as a plain directory - by the time the staged tree exists there is no
    link left to detect. Checking the source catches both shapes (and never writes the bytes).
    A preserved absolute/`..` link would in any case still be followed downstream by `tauri
    build`'s zip/AppImage/`.app` assembly and by P4.41's hash-manifest read, and an out-of-bundle
    link is exactly the offline-floor break G37b catches at release (§0.11 T3a).

    Two shapes are refused, for two different reasons:

    * a link RESOLVING OUTSIDE the tree - the escape proper; and
    * an ABSOLUTE symlink, even one pointing inside its own cache entry, because `copytree`
      preserves a symlink verbatim: the staged copy would still carry the build machine's
      `.engine-cache/` path, which is outside the bundle on the build box and DANGLING on the
      user's (breaking the §3.3.3/§3.9.1 beside-the-exe resolution and SSOT *Portable, no
      installation*). Real engine trees link relatively. A JUNCTION is exempt from this second
      rule - it is absolute by construction and `copytree` materialises it, so no link survives
      into the bundle; only its containment matters. Materialisation does mean an in-tree
      junction's subtree is DUPLICATED in the bundle - a §3.9 size-budget input P5-P7 inherit,
      invisible at the call site, and the reason a junction is never the cheaper option.

    A junction pointing at its own ANCESTOR is refused as a third shape: it passes containment
    (an ancestor is inside the tree) yet makes `copytree`'s materialisation recurse until the
    filesystem refuses. This guard's own walk never descends through a link, so it terminates -
    but the COPY it would authorise does not, and a named refusal beats a generic OSError.
    """
    root = src.resolve()
    stack = [src]
    while stack:
        for path in stack.pop().iterdir():
            junction = path.is_junction()
            if junction or path.is_symlink():
                target = _link_target(path)
                if target != root and root not in target.parents:
                    raise StagingError(
                        f"resource tree '{row.dest}' ({row.cache_engine}): "
                        f"{path.relative_to(src)} is a link resolving outside the staged tree "
                        f"({target}) - a bundled tree may only link within itself "
                        "(§0.11 T3a / the G37b closure)"
                    )
                here = path.parent.resolve()
                if junction and (target == here or target in here.parents):
                    raise StagingError(
                        f"resource tree '{row.dest}' ({row.cache_engine}): "
                        f"{path.relative_to(src)} is a junction pointing at its own ancestor "
                        f"({target}) - `copytree` MATERIALISES a junction, so staging it would "
                        "recurse until the filesystem refuses. A junction may only point at a "
                        "sibling subtree."
                    )
                if not junction and os.path.isabs(os.readlink(path)):
                    raise StagingError(
                        f"resource tree '{row.dest}' ({row.cache_engine}): "
                        f"{path.relative_to(src)} is an ABSOLUTE symlink - `copytree` preserves it "
                        "verbatim, so the staged bundle would carry a build-machine path that "
                        "dangles on the user's machine (§3.3.3 / §3.9.1). Engine trees link "
                        "relatively."
                    )
                continue
            if path.is_dir():
                stack.append(path)


def bundle_entries(conf_path: Path) -> tuple[tuple[str, ...], dict[str, str]]:
    """`bundle.externalBin` and `bundle.resources` out of `tauri.conf.json` (§3.3.1's two shapes).

    Fails CLOSED on an absent or malformed file, unlike the `engines.lock` reader beside it: the
    manifest legitimately declares nothing yet, whereas `tauri.conf.json` is a committed, required
    file and its absence means a broken checkout rather than an empty phase.

    `bundle.resources` is required to be the MAP form. Tauri also accepts a bare list, but §3.3.1
    fixes the map: the engine trees need an explicit bundle-internal target (`engines/libreoffice/`)
    that the list form cannot express, and accepting both would give the binder two shapes to
    reconcile for a spelling the spec does not permit. The narrowing is deliberate, not an
    oversight. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.31]
    """
    try:
        conf = json.loads(conf_path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except OSError as exc:
        raise StagingError(
            f"cannot read the Tauri config at {conf_path} - it is the §3.3.1 bundling declaration "
            "every staged sidecar and resource tree has to appear in",
            structural=True,
        ) from exc
    except json.JSONDecodeError as exc:
        raise StagingError(f"{conf_path} is not valid JSON - {exc}", structural=True) from exc
    bundle = conf.get("bundle")
    if not isinstance(bundle, dict):
        raise StagingError(f"{conf_path}: `bundle` must be an object (§3.3.1)", structural=True)
    # A JSON `null` is how Tauri's own `Option<…>` spells "absent", so it reads as absent here
    # rather than as a type error: refusing it would reject a config Tauri accepts.
    external = bundle.get("externalBin")
    resources = bundle.get("resources")
    external = [] if external is None else external
    resources = {} if resources is None else resources
    if not isinstance(external, list) or not all(isinstance(e, str) for e in external):
        raise StagingError(
            f"{conf_path}: `bundle.externalBin` must be an array of strings (§3.3.1)",
            structural=True,
        )
    if not isinstance(resources, dict) or not all(
        isinstance(k, str) and isinstance(v, str) for k, v in resources.items()
    ):
        raise StagingError(
            f"{conf_path}: `bundle.resources` must be a source->target object of strings (§3.3.1)",
            structural=True,
        )
    return tuple(external), dict(resources)


def _tree_key(name: str) -> str:
    """The ONE spelling of a RESOURCE_TREE row's `bundle.resources` source key (§3.3.1).

    Every site that needs it reads this - both directions of the binder AND the diagnostic that
    tells the author what to write - because the rule having had TWO spellings is exactly what let
    a doubled trailing slash count as a claim on a row the other half simultaneously called
    undeclared. Three literals agreeing today is the same drift one commit away.
    [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.31]
    """
    return f"{BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX}{name}/"


def _means_prefix(path: str, prefix: str) -> bool:
    """Does `path` MEAN `prefix` without spelling it exactly - a capital, a `\\`, a missing slash?

    Only the OWNERSHIP question is asked case- and separator-insensitively; the reported key stays
    verbatim. A Windows-authored `Resources\\libreoffice\\` resolves on the authoring host and
    breaks on the Linux runner, and without this it would leave the check as an unrecognised
    non-engine key rather than as the mistyped destination it is.

    Matched against the prefix WITHOUT its trailing slash, then end-or-separator: the bare
    `resources` spelling means the whole staging tree - Tauri would walk all of it into the
    resource root under the wrong internal paths - while `resources-readme.md` means nothing of
    the sort and stays a non-engine key. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.31]
    """
    normalized = path.replace("\\", "/").lower()
    root = prefix.rstrip("/").lower()
    return normalized == root or normalized.startswith(root + "/")


def unbound_bundle_entries(
    rows: tuple[EngineStaging, ...], external: tuple[str, ...], resources: dict[str, str]
) -> list[str]:
    """Every way a staging row and the §3.3.1 bundle declaration can disagree, in BOTH directions.

    A row with no declaration stages bytes `tauri build` never packages; a declaration with no row
    names a path Tauri looks for and does not find. Both are silent until far downstream, and each
    is a one-token typo away, so both are checked here.

    The `externalBin` entry carries the BARE name under `binaries/` (`binaries/ffmpeg`) - Tauri
    appends the target triple and the `.exe` itself, which is why `sidecar_filename`'s suffix is a
    STAGE-time convention only. Both prefixes are asserted as a SHAPE rule rather than matched
    name-by-name: a bare `"ffmpeg"` entry would resolve `<src-tauri>/ffmpeg-<triple>` and a
    `resources/libreofice/` typo would name a subdir nothing stages, and a check that only compared
    the members either side declares would let the first through and skip the second entirely.

    Which map entries the reverse direction OWNS is the one boundary here: keys under `resources/`
    are the staging step's own destinations (§3.3.1's closed source-key set, §3.3.2 step 3), so an
    unclaimed one is a defect; everything else in the same map is a non-engine resource no staging
    row will ever produce (§3.3.1 lists `THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt`, §3.3.2 step 4 adds the per-build
    SBOM + NOTICE and the §3.4 patent-disposition record), owned by the boxes that generate those
    files. On the sidecar side the same boundary is the NAMED `BUILT_SIDECARS` set rather than a
    prefix. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.31]
    """
    problems: list[str] = []
    sidecars = {row.dest for row in rows if row.kind is StagingKind.SIDECAR}
    trees = {row.dest for row in rows if row.kind is StagingKind.RESOURCE_TREE}
    declared_bins = {
        entry.removeprefix(BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX)
        for entry in external
        if entry.startswith(BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX)
    }
    # ONE derivation - `_tree_key` - read by BOTH directions and by the diagnostic. The reverse
    # check used to `rstrip("/")` its key, which is LOOSER than the forward check's exact spelling:
    # a doubled trailing slash read as a claim on the row while the forward half still called that
    # row undeclared, so the two halves disagreed about what a key is.
    # [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.31]
    claimed_trees = {_tree_key(name) for name in trees}

    problems += [
        f"'{name}' is declared a first-party build product (BUILT_SIDECARS) AND staged by a row - "
        "one of the two is wrong, since the exemption is only for sidecars this script does not "
        "produce"
        for name in sorted(sidecars.intersection(BUILT_SIDECARS))
    ]
    problems += [
        f"sidecar row '{name}' has no `bundle.externalBin` entry (expected "
        f"'{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}{name}')"
        for name in sorted(sidecars - declared_bins)
    ]
    for entry in sorted(set(external)):
        if not entry.startswith(BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX):
            problems.append(
                f"`bundle.externalBin` entry '{entry}' is not under `{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}` - "
                "§3.3.2 step 2 stages every sidecar there, so Tauri would resolve it against a "
                "path nothing writes"
            )
            continue
        name = entry.removeprefix(BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX)
        if name not in sidecars and name not in BUILT_SIDECARS:
            problems.append(f"`bundle.externalBin` entry '{entry}' names no staging row")

    problems += [
        f"resource row '{name}' has no `bundle.resources` key (expected '{_tree_key(name)}')"
        for name in sorted(trees)
        if _tree_key(name) not in resources
    ]
    for key in sorted(resources):
        if key in claimed_trees:
            continue
        if key.startswith(BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX):
            problems.append(f"`bundle.resources` key '{key}' names no staging row")
        elif _means_prefix(key, BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX):
            problems.append(
                f"`bundle.resources` key '{key}' MEANS `{BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX}` but does not spell "
                "it - the source side is case- and separator-sensitive on Linux even where the "
                "authoring host resolves it, and a key that only means it would slip past this "
                "check as a non-engine resource"
            )
    return problems


def assert_rows_bound_to_bundle(rows: tuple[EngineStaging, ...], conf_path: Path) -> None:
    """Fail if the staging rows and §3.3.1's bundle declaration disagree.

    Called from `stage_all`, for the reason its sibling binder states: a binder nothing invokes at
    package time is documentation, not a check. Fail-OPEN while neither side declares anything -
    there is nothing to bind - and fail-CLOSED the moment either side does, in EITHER direction,
    because "staged but not bundled" and "bundled but not staged" are the same typo seen from two
    ends. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.31]
    """
    external, resources = bundle_entries(conf_path)
    problems = unbound_bundle_entries(rows, external, resources)
    if problems:
        raise StagingError(
            "the staging rows and the §3.3.1 bundle declaration disagree (checked against "
            f"{conf_path}):\n  - " + "\n  - ".join(problems)
        )


def assert_rows_bound_to_manifest(rows: tuple[EngineStaging, ...], lock_path: Path) -> None:
    """Fail if a staging row names a cache entry the manifest does not declare.

    Called from `stage_all`, not only from the self-test: a binder nothing invokes at package time
    is documentation, not a check. Fail-OPEN while `engines.lock` declares no rows (there is
    nothing to bind against yet) and fail-CLOSED the moment the first row lands.
    """
    unbound = unbound_staging_rows(rows, lock_path)
    if unbound:
        raise StagingError(
            f"staging row(s) {', '.join(unbound)} name a §6.1.3 cache entry no `engines.lock` "
            f"row declares (checked against {lock_path}). One of the two sides has a typo: the "
            "`cache_engine` token here, or the manifest row the owner added."
        )


def _slice_entries(
    cache_root: Path, row: EngineStaging
) -> dict[str, Path] | None:
    """The row's cache entry for EACH per-arch slice, or None when the engine is absent entirely.

    A row present for one slice and absent for the other is NOT "absent" - it is the case that
    silently ships a half-universal bundle, so it is a refusal that names the missing slice and
    the box that exists to produce it.
    """
    found = {
        slice_triple: select_cache_entry(cache_root, row.cache_engine, slice_triple)
        for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES
    }
    if all(entry is None for entry in found.values()):
        return None
    missing = [slice_triple for slice_triple, entry in found.items() if entry is None]
    if missing:
        raise StagingError(
            f"engine '{row.cache_engine}': the restored cache has "
            f"{', '.join(s for s in UNIVERSAL_SLICES if s not in missing)} but not "
            f"{', '.join(missing)}. A universal artifact needs BOTH slices (§6.1.3 keys them "
            "separately, one per triple) - `lipo` cannot merge a slice it does not have. The "
            "documented fallback is to compile the missing slice from source (P4.29.1): "
            f"`scripts/compile-engine-asset --engine {row.cache_engine} --target {missing[0]}`. "
            # Which of the two that is decides whether it is a CROSS build: the universal leg runs
            # on an arm64 runner, so a missing x86_64 slice is §6.1.3's declared cross fallback
            # while a missing aarch64 slice is an ordinary native compile. Saying "cross-builds"
            # unconditionally would be false on one of the two branches this message is symmetric
            # over. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29.1]
            "On the arm64 universal runner that is "
            f"{'a cross build (§6.1.3 declares the arm64-host to x86_64-target direction)' if missing[0] == UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1] else 'a native build'}. "
            "Either way the row has to declare that triple `from-source` with a configure line - "
            "a prebuilt-declared row gives the compile step nothing to build. This script only "
            "reads the cache; it never compiles."
        )
    # ONE engine is ONE pinned version. `select_cache_entry` is called once per slice and knows
    # nothing about the other, so a stale or half-updated cache - a version bump that landed for
    # one arch's job before the other, cache-key drift, manual cache surgery - would merge 7.1's
    # arm64 slice with 9.9's x86_64 slice into a single file under one name. Nothing downstream
    # can see it: a Mach-O fat header carries no source version, the SBOM lists one, and the two
    # architectures would ship different codec and vulnerability profiles. This is the ONLY point
    # in the pipeline where both version strings are in hand. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29]
    versions = {
        slice_triple: entry.name[len(row.cache_engine) + 1 : -(len(slice_triple) + 1)]
        for slice_triple, entry in found.items()
        if entry is not None
    }
    if len(set(versions.values())) > 1:
        spelled = ", ".join(
            f"{slice_triple}={version or '?'}" for slice_triple, version in versions.items()
        )
        raise StagingError(
            f"engine '{row.cache_engine}': the restored slices pin DIFFERENT versions ({spelled}). "
            "A universal artifact is one engine at one version on two architectures - merging "
            "these would ship a different build to each, under one name, with one SBOM row and no "
            "fat-header field that could ever record the difference."
        )
    return {slice_triple: entry for slice_triple, entry in found.items() if entry is not None}


def merge_universal_tree(
    sources: dict[str, Path], dest: Path, label: str, *, runner, write: bool
) -> None:
    """Merge two per-arch resource trees into one universal tree.

    §6.1.3 says two slices per engine and P10.10 says `stage-engines` reads both restored slices
    "per sidecar/lib" - a resource tree carries libraries too, and shipping one architecture's
    tree in a universal bundle is the same silent crash a thin sidecar is. Every path must exist
    in BOTH trees: a Mach-O pair is lipo'd, a byte-identical pair is copied, and anything else is
    refused rather than guessed at, because a differing non-Mach-O means the two slices disagree
    about something this step has no rule for. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29]
    """
    primary_root, secondary_root = (sources[s] for s in UNIVERSAL_SLICES)

    def relatives(root_dir: Path) -> set[str]:
        """Every path in the tree, DIRECTORIES included, descending exactly as `copytree` does.

        Collecting only files would silently drop a directory both slices carry - an engine's
        empty profile or plugin dir - which the per-arch path preserves via `copytree`. A macOS-
        only asymmetry is exactly the kind nothing else would catch.

        The descent rule is SPELLED here rather than inherited from `rglob`. `rglob` happens to
        walk this way on 3.12 (`_iterate_directories` -> `Path.walk(follow_symlinks=False)`), but
        it is an unstated stdlib default under a parameter that was still being reshaped at 3.13,
        and this file's whole no-harm story rests on the rule: a SYMLINK is preserved, never
        descended (`copytree(symlinks=True)` copies the link itself, so its subtree is not part of
        this tree), while a JUNCTION is descended, because `copytree` keys on `is_symlink()` -
        false for a junction - and MATERIALISES it. Both halves must mirror the per-arch path, or
        the universal build ships a different tree from the three per-arch ones for a reason no
        gate downstream could name. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29]
        """
        out: set[str] = set()
        stack = [root_dir]
        while stack:
            for child in stack.pop().iterdir():
                out.add(child.relative_to(root_dir).as_posix())
                if child.is_dir() and not child.is_symlink():
                    stack.append(child)
        return out

    only_primary = sorted(relatives(primary_root) - relatives(secondary_root))
    only_secondary = sorted(relatives(secondary_root) - relatives(primary_root))
    if only_primary or only_secondary:
        raise StagingError(
            f"{label}: the two per-arch trees do not carry the same paths - "
            f"{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]} has {only_primary[:4] or 'none'} that "
            f"{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]} lacks, and vice versa {only_secondary[:4] or 'none'}. A "
            "universal tree is one tree with fat binaries in it, not two trees merged by guess."
        )
    # NOT `if not write: return` here. Every check below is a VERDICT on the restored cache,
    # not a consequence of writing, and a --check that returned at the path-set diff validated
    # strictly less than the real run: it passed trees carrying identical-thin Mach-Os,
    # dir-vs-file and symlink-vs-file divergences and differing non-Mach-Os that the real run
    # refuses. A dry run that is greener than the wet one is the one thing a check mode must
    # never be, so every verdict runs unconditionally and `write` gates only the statements that
    # CREATE something - the directories, and the ones that place content. Stated as the RULE
    # rather than as a count: the count was wrong within one round of being written (r5's
    # empty-tree `dest.mkdir` landed directly beneath it), which is the count-then-enumerate shape
    # this commit closes elsewhere. The single verdict --check cannot reach is `lipo`'s own, which
    # needs the tool.
    # [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29]
    if write:
        # The destination exists even when both slices are EMPTY. `copytree` on the per-arch path
        # creates it unconditionally, and `relatives()`'s contract is that the universal tree does
        # not differ from the three per-arch ones - a corner, but the corner that contract names.
        dest.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    for rel in sorted(relatives(primary_root)):
        primary, secondary = primary_root / rel, secondary_root / rel
        target = dest / rel
        if write:
            target.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        # BOTH sides checked for a link: `is_dir()` follows a symlink, so a real directory in
        # one slice and a symlink-to-directory in the other would take this branch and publish a
        # plain empty directory, silently dropping the other slice's layout.
        if (
            primary.is_dir()
            and secondary.is_dir()
            and not primary.is_symlink()
            and not secondary.is_symlink()
        ):
            if write:
                target.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            continue
        if primary.is_dir() != secondary.is_dir():
            raise StagingError(
                f"{label}: '{rel}' is a directory in one slice and a file in the other"
            )
        if primary.is_symlink() or secondary.is_symlink():
            if not (primary.is_symlink() and secondary.is_symlink()):
                # Naming the other side's REAL type, because two different divergences reach this
                # one guard - a symlink against a plain file, and a symlink against a real
                # directory - and a shared message lets either leg pass on the other's input.
                linked, plain = (
                    (UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0], secondary)
                    if primary.is_symlink()
                    else (UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1], primary)
                )
                raise StagingError(
                    f"{label}: '{rel}' is a symlink in {linked} and a real "
                    f"{'directory' if plain.is_dir() else 'file'} in the other slice"
                )
            if os.readlink(primary) != os.readlink(secondary):
                raise StagingError(
                    f"{label}: the two slices point '{rel}' at different targets"
                )
            if write:
                target.symlink_to(os.readlink(primary))
        elif same_bytes(primary, secondary):
            # Byte-identity FIRST: an already-universal member is byte-identical in both slices
            # and carries a fat magic, so testing `is_macho` first would hand `lipo -create` two
            # copies of the same fat binary - which it refuses for overlapping architectures.
            #
            # But identity is not evidence of FATNESS: two slices can carry the same THIN (or
            # arm64-only fat) Mach-O - a copied `engines.lock` row, P4.29.1's cross-compile
            # quietly emitting a native slice, a poisoned cache - and copying that verbatim ships
            # a single-arch library that G30 cannot see, because it finds universal artifacts by
            # NAME and a tree member is called `libfoo.dylib`. So a Mach-O is asserted even on the
            # path that does not merge it. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29]
            if is_macho(primary):
                assert_universal_fat(primary, f"{label}: {rel}")
            if write:
                shutil.copy2(primary, target)
        elif is_macho(primary) and is_macho(secondary):
            if write:
                merge_universal_binary(
                    [primary, secondary],
                    target,
                    f"{label}: {rel}",
                    runner=runner,
                    # A tree carries libraries AND launchers (§3.3.1 stages LibreOffice's inside
                    # `resources/`), so the merged member has to keep the mode its slice had -
                    # otherwise the universal build alone ships a non-executable launcher. Applied
                    # through `finalize`, i.e. on the staging file: see merge_universal_binary.
                    finalize=lambda staged, src=primary: shutil.copystat(src, staged),
                )
        elif is_macho(primary) or is_macho(secondary):
            # BOTH or neither. Handing `lipo` a Mach-O and a data file produces an unhelpful
            # tool error for what is really a packaging divergence between the two slices, and
            # `or` would have done exactly that.
            macho_side = UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0] if is_macho(primary) else UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]
            raise StagingError(
                f"{label}: '{rel}' is a Mach-O in {macho_side} and not in the other slice - the "
                "two slices disagree about what this file IS, which is a packaging divergence, "
                "not something to merge"
            )
        else:
            raise StagingError(
                f"{label}: '{rel}' differs between the two slices but is not a Mach-O, so there "
                "is no merge rule for it - a universal tree cannot carry two versions of one file"
            )


def stage_universal(
    root: Path,
    cache_root: Path,
    rows: tuple[EngineStaging, ...],
    *,
    write: bool,
    runner,
) -> list[StagedResult]:
    """Stage every row for `universal-apple-darwin` by merging both per-arch slices.

    Ordering note (§6.1.3 / the P4.29 box): `lipo` runs FIRST, the P4.30 install_name relocation tail
    afterwards - `lipo -create` re-emits the Mach-O and rewrites its load commands, so a
    relocation applied to a per-arch slice would be clobbered by the merge.
    """
    for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
        for name, claimants in ambiguous_engine_entries(cache_root, rows, slice_triple):
            raise StagingError(
                f"restored cache entry '{name}' parses as belonging to more than one declared "
                f"engine ({', '.join(claimants)}) - the "
                "`<cache_engine>-<cache_version>-<triple>` key must identify exactly one engine "
                "(§6.1.3)"
            )
    clear_resource_destinations(root, rows, write=write)
    results: list[StagedResult] = []
    for row in rows:
        entries = _slice_entries(cache_root, row)
        if entries is None:
            results.append(StagedResult(row, None, None))
            continue
        primary = entries[UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]]
        if row.kind is StagingKind.SIDECAR:
            members = {}
            for slice_triple, entry in entries.items():
                src = _member_path(entry, row.member or row.dest)
                if not src.is_file():
                    raise StagingError(
                        f"sidecar '{row.dest}': {src} is missing from the restored "
                        f"{slice_triple} cache entry {entry.name}"
                    )
                members[slice_triple] = src
            dest = sidecar_dest(root, row.dest, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE)
            slices = [members[s] for s in UNIVERSAL_SLICES]
            # Structured like merge_universal_tree and for the same reason: every VERDICT below is
            # a judgement on the restored cache, not a consequence of writing, so it runs in BOTH
            # modes and only the byte-moving statements are gated on `write`. The sidecar was the
            # sibling that rule was NOT applied to when the tree half was restructured - `--check`
            # signed off a thin already-universal sidecar the real run refuses, the same
            # dry-run-greener-than-the-wet-one defect one function down. `ci.yml`'s check pass
            # runs `--check` for the RUNNER's host triple, so it does not reach this branch today;
            # the universal target gets its live check consumer at P10.10, and a check mode that
            # validates less than the run is a defect the moment it does.
            # [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29]
            if same_bytes(slices[0], slices[1]):
                # An upstream that already publishes a universal binary lands under BOTH slice
                # keys, and `lipo -create` refuses two copies of one fat file. Handled the same
                # way the tree path handles it, rather than dying on a raw tool error.
                #
                # Asserted UNCONDITIONALLY here, and deliberately not behind the `is_macho` test
                # the tree path uses: a sidecar IS the executable Tauri slots into externalBin
                # under the universal name, never an arch-independent data file the way a tree
                # member can be - it is precisely the artifact G30 looks for BY that name
                # (§6.1.3), so a non-Mach-O one is a defect wherever it came from.
                assert_universal_fat(slices[0], f"sidecar '{row.dest}'")
                if write:
                    # Atomic for the same reason the merge is: a torn write under the
                    # universal name survives a failed run, and a later run whose engine is
                    # simply absent from the cache exits 0 with it still there.
                    dest.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
                    staged_copy = dest.with_name(f".{dest.name}.merging")
                    staged_copy.unlink(missing_ok=True)
                    try:
                        shutil.copy2(slices[0], staged_copy)
                        _set_exec_bit(staged_copy)
                        os.replace(staged_copy, dest)
                    finally:
                        staged_copy.unlink(missing_ok=True)
            elif not (is_macho(slices[0]) and is_macho(slices[1])):
                # The sidecar twin of the tree path's Mach-O-asymmetry refusal. `lipo -create`
                # does fail on this input, but as a raw tool error naming neither the row nor the
                # slice - and `--check`, which never runs `lipo`, passed it outright.
                spelled = ", ".join(
                    f"{slice_triple}={'Mach-O' if is_macho(member) else 'not a Mach-O'}"
                    for slice_triple, member in zip(UNIVERSAL_SLICES, slices)
                )
                raise StagingError(
                    f"sidecar '{row.dest}': the two slices differ but are not both Mach-O "
                    f"({spelled}), so there is nothing for `lipo -create` to merge - a universal "
                    "sidecar is one executable on two architectures (§6.1.3)"
                )
            elif write:
                merge_universal_binary(
                    slices,
                    dest,
                    f"sidecar '{row.dest}'",
                    runner=runner,
                    # On the STAGING file, not afterwards: `lipo -create` writes its own mode, and
                    # a run interrupted between the move and a later chmod leaves a fat but
                    # non-executable sidecar wearing the final name - which the next
                    # engine-absent run keeps, the very scenario the staging sibling exists for.
                    finalize=_set_exec_bit,
                )
        else:
            trees = {}
            for slice_triple, entry in entries.items():
                src = _member_path(entry, row.member)
                if not src.is_dir():
                    raise StagingError(
                        f"resource tree '{row.dest}': {src} is missing from the restored "
                        f"{slice_triple} cache entry {entry.name} (a `bundle.resources` row "
                        "stages a DIRECTORY, §3.3.1)"
                    )
                assert_no_escaping_links(src, row)
                trees[slice_triple] = src
            dest = resource_dest(root, row.dest)
            merge_universal_tree(
                trees, dest, f"resource tree '{row.dest}'", runner=runner, write=write
            )
        results.append(StagedResult(row, primary, dest))
    return results


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The §3.5 beside-the-exe load-path rewrite (P4.30). Three INDEPENDENT per-OS legs: each is a
# different toolchain on a different CI leg, so a macOS `install_name_tool` failure must not read
# as a Linux `patchelf` failure. Keyed on the TRIPLE, never the host - a cross-named stage has to
# get the target's rewrite, the same rule `_WINDOWS_TRIPLE_MARKER` already holds for the suffix.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def _macho_prefix(rel: Relocation) -> str:
    """The `@loader_path` prefix a macOS load path carries. ONE spelling, shared by the builder
    and by the expectation that reads its result back - two independent spellings of one rule is
    the enumeration-not-rule shape this box spent a review round removing elsewhere, and a
    divergence between them would fail closed (a false refusal) rather than loudly.
    [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]"""
    return "@loader_path" if rel.origin == "." else f"@loader_path/{rel.origin}"


def _macho_relocation_argv(path: Path, rel: Relocation) -> list[list[str]]:
    """P4.30.1 - macOS. `install_name_tool`, one invocation carrying every declared operation.

    `-id @loader_path/<name>` rewrites a shipped lib's OWN install name, which is what a dependent
    binary records when it links against it; `-change <abs> @loader_path/<lib>` rewrites the
    ABSOLUTE entries an upstream build already baked into the dependent. One invocation because
    the tool takes them together and applies them atomically - a half-rewritten Mach-O is not a
    state worth being able to reach. Runs over the LIPO'D fat binary, never a per-arch slice:
    `lipo -create` re-emits the Mach-O and rewrites its load commands, so a rewrite applied first
    would be clobbered (the P4.29 ordering note, which `stage_universal`'s docstring also carries).
    """
    prefix = _macho_prefix(rel)
    operations: list[str] = []
    if rel.set_id:
        operations += ["-id", f"{prefix}/{path.name}"]
    for absolute, bundled in rel.references:
        operations += ["-change", absolute, f"{prefix}/{bundled}"]
    return [["install_name_tool", *operations, str(path)]] if operations else []


def _elf_relocation_argv(path: Path, rel: Relocation) -> list[list[str]]:
    """P4.30.2 - Linux. `patchelf --set-rpath '$ORIGIN'` so `DT_RUNPATH` resolves beside the exe.

    `$ORIGIN` is expanded by the loader against the DIRECTORY OF THE BINARY ITSELF, which is
    exactly the beside-the-exe layout §3.9.1 prefers. Passed as a literal argv token - `shell=False`
    means the shell never sees the `$`, which is the whole reason it survives to `patchelf`.
    Set unconditionally, with no `set_id`/`references` analogue: ELF has no per-lib install name to
    rewrite, so on this platform the search path IS the entire mechanism.

    TWO properties of `DT_RUNPATH` the declaring box has to know, because neither is visible from
    this argv (§6.1.3's Linux sub-bullet carries both):
      * it is **not transitive** - `ld.so` consults it only for the DIRECT dependencies of the
        object carrying it - so a chain like `ffmpeg -> libvorbisenc -> libvorbis -> libogg` needs
        a `Relocation` row for EVERY link, not just the executable. The mechanism supports that
        (rows are per file); nothing here can enforce it.
      * it is consulted **after** `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`, where the legacy `DT_RPATH` precedes it. So
        the beside-the-exe guarantee on Linux rests on §3.5's loader-variable strip; `--force-rpath`
        would add a second line and is deliberately NOT taken, because `DT_RPATH` is deprecated and
        the strip is a control this repo already holds and tests.
    """
    rpath = "$ORIGIN" if rel.origin == "." else f"$ORIGIN/{rel.origin}"
    return [["patchelf", "--set-rpath", rpath, str(path)]]


def _pe_relocation_argv(path: Path, rel: Relocation) -> list[list[str]]:
    """P4.30.3 - Windows. A RECORDED no-op, declared so the absence is a decision and not a gap.

    The PE loader searches the directory of the executable that started the process before any
    other user path, so a DLL shipped beside the exe already resolves with nothing rewritten -
    there is no PE analogue of `install_name`/`RUNPATH` to fix. The minimal explicit `PATH`
    Windows engines are spawned with - the bundle dir only, so the OS search STARTS inside the
    bundle - is §0.11 T3a's control (asserted by G37b), not §3.5's, which says only that `PATH` is
    not relied on. The G37b closure assertion's Windows arm (`dumpbin /dependents`) therefore
    validates the AS-STAGED layout directly, with no rewrite step in between.

    It returns no argv rather than being absent from the dispatch table: an unlisted triple is a
    REFUSAL (`relocator_for`), so declaring the no-op is what distinguishes "nothing to do here"
    from "nobody thought about here".
    """
    return []


#: triple -> the argv builder for that target's load-path rewrite. Every §3.4.5 v1 triple plus the
#: universal merge target is listed; `relocator_for` refuses anything else rather than defaulting
#: to a no-op, so a widened `V1_TARGET_TRIPLES` cannot arrive silently unrelocated. A leg pins the
#: coverage in both directions.
_RELOCATORS = {
    "x86_64-pc-windows-msvc": _pe_relocation_argv,
    "aarch64-apple-darwin": _macho_relocation_argv,
    "x86_64-apple-darwin": _macho_relocation_argv,
    UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE: _macho_relocation_argv,
    "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu": _elf_relocation_argv,
}

#: triple -> the READ-BACK argv builders for the two halves a rewrite can have: `id` (this file's
#: own install name) and `refs` (the references it makes). `None` where a platform rewrites nothing.
#: Linux's `--print-rpath` answers the whole mechanism in one call, so both halves point at it;
#: macOS needs two tools, `otool -D` for the install name and `otool -L` for the references.
#:
#: The `refs` half is NOT G37b arriving early. G37b asserts CLOSURE - every load command, `LC_RPATH`
#: included, resolves inside the bundle - which needs `otool -l` and a per-binary dependency walk.
#: This asserts only that the PAIRS THIS ROW DECLARED were actually applied, over data the
#: `Relocation` already carries. It matters because `install_name_tool -change <old> <new>` matches
#: `<old>` LITERALLY and, on no match, does nothing and exits 0 - so the dependent binary, the one
#: case with no `-id` to read back, was the half where the silent-no-op class was still open.
_RELOCATION_READBACK = {
    "x86_64-pc-windows-msvc": None,
    "aarch64-apple-darwin": {"id": "otool -D", "refs": "otool -L"},
    "x86_64-apple-darwin": {"id": "otool -D", "refs": "otool -L"},
    UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE: {"id": "otool -D", "refs": "otool -L"},
    # ONE tool answers the whole Linux mechanism, so both halves name it rather than one being
    # dead data nothing reads (a mutation proved the `refs` entry unreachable there).
    "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu": dict.fromkeys(("id", "refs"), "patchelf --print-rpath"),
}


def scope_ids_still_absent(listed, decided) -> bool:
    """Is every piece the SCOPE sentence lists as excluded still absent from this file?

    A pure predicate so the COMPARISON is drivable: with no listed id currently decided here, a leg
    that only evaluates the live inputs cannot tell a working comparison from a dropped one - which
    a mutation proved. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]
    """
    return bool(listed) and not (set(listed) & set(decided))


def build_relocation_argv(path: Path, rel: Relocation, triple: str) -> list[list[str]]:
    """The rewrite argv for one file, with the contract `relocate_one` depends on asserted.

    The path is the LAST token, and `relocate_one` substitutes exactly that token to point at its
    staging sibling. A builder that put it anywhere else would leave the real target argument
    pointing at the PUBLISHED file, so the tool would rewrite it in place - silently losing the
    atomic publish. Asserted rather than trusted, because the three current builders satisfy it
    only by construction.

    Separated from `relocate_one` so `--check` reaches it too: the contract is byte-free, and a
    check pass that skipped it would validate LESS than the run for a reason that has nothing to do
    with the bytes. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]
    """
    argvs = relocator_for(triple)(path, rel)
    for argv in argvs:
        if argv[-1] != str(path):
            raise StagingError(
                f"relocating '{path.name}': the argv builder for '{triple}' does not end in the "
                f"target path ({argv[-1]!r}) - `relocate_one` substitutes the LAST token to point "
                "at its staging sibling, so a builder that places the path elsewhere would rewrite "
                "the published file in place and lose the atomic publish"
            )
    return argvs


def assert_not_symlink(path, label: str) -> None:
    """Refuse a relocation target that is a SYMLINK, before anything reads or resolves it.

    `shutil.copy2` follows links, so the copy-rewrite-replace would silently FLATTEN the link into
    a real file - and a resource tree is staged with `copytree(symlinks=True)` precisely because a
    real LibreOffice/`.app` tree is full of internal links, which the staging guards keep (they
    refuse only links that ESCAPE the tree). Which of the two things a rewrite should mean there -
    edit the link's target, or replace the link - is the declaring box's call, not this mechanism's.

    Called on the RAW path, BEFORE `_member_path`, because `_member_path` resolves: after it, a
    symlinked member is indistinguishable from its target and this guard could never fire on the
    one case class it exists for. It takes only `is_symlink()`, so a leg drives it with a stub and
    no OS link privilege. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]
    """
    if path.is_symlink():
        raise StagingError(
            f"relocating '{label}': the target is a SYMLINK. Rewriting it would either flatten the "
            "link into a real file or edit whatever it points at, and neither is a choice this "
            "mechanism may make for the box that declared the relocation - name the real file"
        )


def relocator_for(triple: str):
    """The argv builder for `triple`, or a structural refusal - never a silent no-op."""
    builder = _RELOCATORS.get(triple)
    if builder is None:
        raise StagingError(
            f"no declared load-path relocation for target '{triple}' - every §3.4.5 v1 triple and "
            f"the universal merge target declare one ({', '.join(sorted(_RELOCATORS))}), because a "
            "target with no declared rewrite would stage binaries carrying the build machine's "
            "ABSOLUTE load paths and only fail on the user's machine (§3.5)",
            structural=True,
        )
    return builder


def assert_relocations_bound(
    rows: tuple[EngineStaging, ...], relocations: tuple[Relocation, ...]
) -> None:
    """Refuse a relocation declaring a `dest` no staging row stages.

    The binder's load-bearing direction: a typo'd `dest` sits there looking authoritative while the
    real binary is staged with its absolute paths intact and fails on the user's machine. The
    inverse direction is deliberately NOT checked - a staged row with no relocation is the NORMAL
    case (a statically-linked worker, a data tree, the Windows no-op), so requiring one would be
    the wrong default. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]
    """
    staged = {row.dest for row in rows}
    unbound = sorted({rel.dest for rel in relocations if rel.dest not in staged})
    if unbound:
        raise StagingError(
            f"relocation declaration(s) for {', '.join(unbound)} name no staged row - a rewrite "
            "bound to nothing never runs, and the binary it was meant to fix ships with the build "
            "machine's absolute load paths"
        )


def relocate_one(path: Path, rel: Relocation, triple: str, *, runner) -> list[list[str]]:
    """Rewrite ONE staged file's load paths in place, atomically. Returns the argv that ran.

    ATOMIC, like every other publish in this file: the rewrite runs against a `.{name}.relocating`
    sibling and the finished file is moved into place with one `os.replace`, so a crashed or
    failing tool leaves the staged binary exactly as it was rather than half-rewritten. Rewriting
    the published file directly would be simpler and would re-open precisely the torn-publish
    window the P4.29 legs pin shut. The tool is told the FINAL basename explicitly (it is an
    argument, not something read off the file), so the temp name never leaks into an install name.

    WHAT THIS ASSERTS, and what it does not. It asserts the tool succeeded, that the result is
    still a non-empty file of the SAME format it started as (a rewrite that truncates or corrupts
    its input is caught here, at the step that produced it - the P4.29 discipline), and, where the
    toolchain can answer precisely, it READS THE RESULT BACK: `patchelf --print-rpath` reports the
    whole Linux mechanism, `otool -D` reports the macOS install name. It does NOT assert dependency
    CLOSURE - that every reference resolves inside the bundle is G37b's assertion, built per engine
    and run at release over these relocated binaries, and building it here would be this box
    claiming a gate the plan gives to another.
    """
    assert_not_symlink(path, path.name)
    argvs = build_relocation_argv(path, rel, triple)
    if not argvs:
        return []
    with path.open("rb") as handle:
        before = handle.read(4)
    staging = path.with_name(f".{path.name}.relocating")
    try:
        shutil.copy2(path, staging)
        for argv in argvs:
            step = [*argv[:-1], str(staging)]
            result = runner(step)
            if getattr(result, "returncode", 1) != 0:
                raise StagingError(
                    f"relocating '{path.name}': `{' '.join(step)}` failed:\n"
                    f"{getattr(result, 'stderr', '') or getattr(result, 'stdout', '')}".strip()
                )
        if not staging.is_file() or staging.stat().st_size == 0:
            raise StagingError(
                f"relocating '{path.name}': the rewrite left no usable file - a load-path rewrite "
                "edits headers in place and must never shorten its input to nothing"
            )
        with staging.open("rb") as handle:
            after = handle.read(4)
        if after != before:
            raise StagingError(
                f"relocating '{path.name}': the rewrite changed the file's FORMAT (its leading "
                "magic differs) - a load-path rewrite edits load commands, never the container"
            )
        _verify_relocation(staging, expected_load_paths(path.name, rel, triple), runner=runner)
        shutil.copystat(path, staging)
        os.replace(staging, path)
    finally:
        # No `exists()` guard: it is redundant before `missing_ok=True` and strictly
        # WEAKER - a broken-symlink residue answers False and would survive the cleanup.
        staging.unlink(missing_ok=True)
    return argvs


def expected_load_paths(final_name: str, rel: Relocation,
                        triple: str) -> list[tuple[str, str, str]]:
    """The `(read-back tool, value it must contain)` pairs this rewrite is checkable by.

    A pure function of the FINAL basename - the file being rewritten still wears its
    `.{name}.relocating` staging name, and matching on that would pin the temp spelling into the
    assertion. Empty where the platform rewrites nothing. Every DECLARED operation contributes an
    expectation: the `-id` half is read back with `otool -D`, and each `-change` pair with
    `otool -L`, because `install_name_tool -change` matches its old path literally and silently
    exits 0 when it does not match - the shape a rewrite is most likely to fail in. Linux's
    `--print-rpath` answers the whole mechanism in one value. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]
    """
    tools = _RELOCATION_READBACK.get(triple)
    if tools is None:
        return []
    if triple == "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu":
        return [(tools["id"], "$ORIGIN" if rel.origin == "." else f"$ORIGIN/{rel.origin}", "")]
    prefix = _macho_prefix(rel)
    expected: list[tuple[str, str, str]] = []
    if rel.set_id:
        expected.append((tools["id"], f"{prefix}/{final_name}", ""))
    # The third element is what must NOT survive. For a `-change` pair the old ABSOLUTE path is
    # the whole point of the rewrite, so asserting its presence is gone closes the silent-no-op
    # class a notch deeper than asserting the new value appeared: the new value could already be
    # there from an unrelated load command.
    expected += [(tools["refs"], f"{prefix}/{bundled}", absolute)
                 for absolute, bundled in rel.references]
    return expected


def _verify_relocation(path: Path, expected: list[tuple[str, str, str]], *, runner) -> None:
    """Read the rewrite back through the toolchain, once per DECLARED operation.

    Exit-code-only would let an `install_name_tool` that silently does nothing publish a binary
    still carrying the build machine's paths - the exact silent class this repo keeps closing one
    step upstream of the gate that would otherwise find it. Each tool is run once even when several
    expectations share it, so a five-reference dependent costs one `otool -L`, not five.
    """
    for tool in dict.fromkeys(cmd for cmd, _, _ in expected):
        argv = [*tool.split(), str(path)]
        result = runner(argv)
        if getattr(result, "returncode", 1) != 0:
            raise StagingError(
                f"relocating '{path.name}': the rewrite reported success but could not be read "
                f"back (`{' '.join(argv)}` failed) - an unverifiable rewrite is not a rewrite"
            )
        # Matched on whitespace-delimited TOKENS, not as a substring: every one of these tools
        # prints one path per whitespace-separated field, and a substring test would accept
        # `$ORIGINAL/lib` for `$ORIGIN` or `…dylib.2` for `…dylib`.
        seen = getattr(result, "stdout", "") or ""
        tokens = seen.split()
        for cmd, wanted, forbidden in expected:
            if cmd != tool:
                continue
            if wanted not in tokens:
                raise StagingError(
                    f"relocating '{path.name}': the tool exited 0 but a load path it should have "
                    f"written is absent - expected {wanted!r} in `{tool}`, got {seen.strip()!r}"
                )
            if forbidden and forbidden in tokens:
                raise StagingError(
                    f"relocating '{path.name}': the tool exited 0 and the new load path is there, "
                    f"but the ABSOLUTE one it was meant to replace ({forbidden!r}) is still "
                    f"present in `{tool}` - the rewrite did not take"
                )


def relocate_all(
    results: list[StagedResult],
    triple: str,
    relocations: tuple[Relocation, ...] | None = None,
    *,
    write: bool,
    runner,
) -> list[tuple[Path, list[list[str]]]]:
    """Rewrite every declared staged file's load paths. Runs AFTER staging, over the published file.

    After, not during: on macOS the file to rewrite is the one `lipo -create` produced, and lipo
    re-emits the Mach-O with fresh load commands (the P4.29 ordering note). Doing it in a tail also
    keeps the rewrite off the four publish paths that carry no `finalize` hook, so every staged
    file is reachable by the same rule rather than by which branch happened to stage it.

    `write=False` (--check) resolves and validates everything that does not need the bytes - the
    declaration binds to a staged row, the triple has a relocator, the target path resolves, the
    argv is constructible - and rewrites nothing. It deliberately does not go quiet: a check pass
    that validated LESS than the run would be the one shape a check mode must never have.

    ORDERING, for the next box that adds a tail here: the P4.41 in-bundle hash manifest is
    generated over the STAGED binaries and must run AFTER this rewrite, on BOTH `stage_all`
    branches - a manifest taken before it records hashes no shipped file has, which would surface
    only at P6.1.2 as a runtime "integrity failed" on every user machine. This file already
    carries the same ordering hazard twice for lipo-vs-relocation, and this commit's own mutation
    battery proved a one-branch tail is a live, suite-invisible defect.
    """
    # Resolved HERE, not as a parameter default: `= RELOCATIONS` in the signature binds the value
    # at DEFINITION time, so the empty tuple this module ships would be frozen into the default and
    # a declaration set afterwards would never reach the tail. The wiring leg found exactly that.
    # [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]
    relocations = RELOCATIONS if relocations is None else relocations
    relocator_for(triple)  # refuse an unrelocatable target before resolving a single path
    if not relocations:
        return []
    # SEVERAL rows may share one RESOURCE_TREE destination (`EngineStaging`'s own docstring: §3.3.1
    # maps the whole image stack onto `resources/image/`), and they come from DIFFERENT cache
    # engines, so their presence is independent. A last-wins dict would let one absent engine hide
    # a destination another engine did stage - the relocation would be skipped while its target sat
    # on disk. Prefer the row that actually staged. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]
    staged: dict[str, StagedResult] = {}
    for result in results:
        seen = staged.get(result.row.dest)
        if seen is None or (seen.dest is None and result.dest is not None):
            staged[result.row.dest] = result
    planned: list[tuple[Path, list[list[str]]]] = []
    for rel in relocations:
        result = staged.get(rel.dest)
        if result is None or result.dest is None:
            # The engine is not in the restored cache - `report` already says so for the row, and a
            # rewrite of a file that was never staged is not a violation, it is nothing to do.
            continue
        if result.row.kind is StagingKind.SIDECAR and rel.member:
            raise StagingError(
                f"relocation for sidecar '{rel.dest}' names a member {rel.member!r} - a sidecar "
                "row stages exactly one file, so its relocation addresses that file and nothing "
                "inside it"
            )
        if result.row.kind is StagingKind.SIDECAR and rel.set_id:
            # A sidecar is staged under the externalBin STAGE-TIME name `<dest>-<triple>[.exe]`,
            # which Tauri STRIPS at bundle time (the module docstring's own note) - so an install
            # name derived from `path.name` here would bake a filename that does not ship, and the
            # read-back would then confirm that wrong value, satisfying the silent-no-op guard WITH
            # the defect. `set_id` belongs on a component lib in the resource tree, whose staged
            # basename IS its shipped basename. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]
            raise StagingError(
                f"relocation for sidecar '{rel.dest}' asks for `set_id`, but a sidecar is staged "
                f"as '{Path(result.dest).name}' and ships under the bare name Tauri strips that "
                "suffix down to - an install name derived from the staged name would point at a "
                "file that does not exist in the bundle. `set_id` addresses a component lib in the "
                "resource tree, whose staged basename is its shipped basename"
            )
        raw = (result.dest / rel.member) if rel.member else result.dest
        assert_not_symlink(raw, rel.dest)
        target = _member_path(result.dest, rel.member) if rel.member else result.dest
        if not write:
            planned.append((target, build_relocation_argv(target, rel, triple)))
            continue
        if not target.is_file():
            raise StagingError(
                f"relocation for '{rel.dest}' names {target.name}, which the staging step did not "
                "produce - the declaration and the staged tree disagree about what ships"
            )
        planned.append((target, relocate_one(target, rel, triple, runner=runner)))
    # REPORTED, not silent: this box's whole thesis is that a rewrite which quietly does nothing is
    # the failure mode worth catching, so a run that rewrote zero files must not look like a run
    # that rewrote three. `report()` does not carry relocation rows, so the count is said here.
    #
    # Counted on the rows that actually PRODUCED argv, not on the rows considered - on the Windows
    # recorded no-op every builder returns `[]`, and reporting "relocated 1" there would say exactly
    # the thing the sentence above forbids, on the one platform whose entire point is that its
    # no-op be distinguishable. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]
    rewritten = [target for target, argvs in planned if argvs]
    if rewritten:
        note(f"{'would relocate' if not write else 'relocated'} {len(rewritten)} staged file(s) "
             f"for {triple}")
    elif planned:
        note(f"{len(planned)} staged file(s) declared for {triple} need no load-path rewrite "
             "(the platform's recorded no-op)")
    return planned


def stage_all(
    root: Path,
    cache_root: Path,
    triple: str,
    rows: tuple[EngineStaging, ...] = STAGED_ENGINES,
    *,
    write: bool = True,
    lock_path: Path | None = None,
    conf_path: Path | None = None,
    runner=None,
) -> list[StagedResult]:
    """Stage every declared row for `triple` from `cache_root` into `root`.

    `write=False` is the --check / --dry-run path: everything is resolved and validated (a missing
    member, an ambiguous pin, an entry two declared engines both claim all still fail), nothing is
    copied. An entry no row claims is informational, not a failure - see unclaimed_cache_entries.
    """
    if lock_path is not None:
        assert_rows_bound_to_manifest(rows, lock_path)
    if conf_path is not None:
        assert_rows_bound_to_bundle(rows, conf_path)
    assert_relocations_bound(rows, RELOCATIONS)
    # Defaulted ONCE, here, rather than inside the universal branch: since P4.30 the per-arch
    # branch spawns tools too (the relocation tail), so a default that lived on one branch would
    # leave the other calling `None`. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]
    runner = runner or run_tool

    if not cache_root.is_dir():
        raise StagingError(
            f"engine-asset cache root {cache_root} does not exist - it is restored by the "
            "workflow's `actions/cache` step (P4.28) and is never fetched from the network here",
            structural=True,
        )
    if triple == UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE:
        results = stage_universal(root, cache_root, rows, write=write, runner=runner)
        relocate_all(results, triple, write=write, runner=runner)
        return results
    for name, claimants in ambiguous_engine_entries(cache_root, rows, triple):
        raise StagingError(
            f"restored cache entry '{name}' parses as belonging to more than one declared engine "
            f"({', '.join(claimants)}) - the `<cache_engine>-<cache_version>-<triple>` key must identify "
            "exactly one engine (§6.1.3)"
        )
    clear_resource_destinations(root, rows, write=write)
    results: list[StagedResult] = []
    for row in rows:
        entry = select_cache_entry(cache_root, row.cache_engine, triple)
        if entry is None:
            results.append(StagedResult(row, None, None))
            continue
        if row.kind is StagingKind.SIDECAR:
            dest = stage_sidecar(entry, row, triple, root, write=write)
        else:
            dest = stage_resource_tree(entry, row, root, write=write)
        results.append(StagedResult(row, entry, dest))
    relocate_all(results, triple, write=write, runner=runner)
    return results


def report(
    results: list[StagedResult],
    triple: str,
    *,
    write: bool,
    verbose: bool,
    unclaimed: list[str] | None = None,
) -> None:
    staged = [r for r in results if r.dest is not None]
    absent = [r for r in results if r.dest is None]
    verb = "staged" if write else "resolved"
    if not results:
        note(
            f"no engine staging rows declared yet for {triple} - STAGED_ENGINES is filled by the "
            "P5-P7 per-engine staging boxes; the mechanism is live"
        )
        return
    note(f"{verb} {len(staged)}/{len(results)} declared engines for {triple}")
    if verbose:
        for r in staged:
            print(f"  {r.row.kind.value:<13} {r.row.dest:<22} <- {r.entry.name if r.entry else ''}")
        for name in unclaimed or []:
            print(f"  unclaimed      {name} (a build/link input, or a missing staging row)")
    for r in absent:
        note(f"not in the restored cache for {triple}: {r.row.cache_engine} ({r.row.kind.value})")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --selftest: the fixture-driven regression suite (see the module docstring's decision note).
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

_results: list[tuple[str, bool]] = []


def _record(name: str, ok: bool) -> None:
    _results.append((name, ok))
    print(f"[{'PASS' if ok else 'FAIL'}] {name}")


def _raises(fn, needle: str) -> bool:
    """Did `fn` fail with a StagingError whose message contains `needle`?

    The needle is REQUIRED, deliberately. Several distinct violations share this one exception
    type, so a type-only assertion can be satisfied by a different arm than the leg names - which
    is exactly how the binder-integration leg below passed while the binder was disconnected (a
    stale sibling fixture made `stage_all` raise from the member-resolution arm instead). Making
    the needle mandatory closes the CLASS rather than that one leg: an arm-blind leg cannot be
    written any more.

    A WRONG exception type is a named FAIL, never a dead suite. It used to propagate, so a guard
    whose removal made the call fail some other way (an `AttributeError` from a stub that only
    needed to answer the guard) killed the whole run with a traceback instead of reddening the one
    leg that names the guard - loud either way, but attributable only in the second form. The
    sibling `compile-engine-asset._raises` already has exactly this shape (`except kind` then
    `except Exception: return False`), and the g24 canaries' `_refused` carries the reason in so
    many words - "a named FAIL beats a dead canary".
    [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]
    """
    try:
        fn()
    except StagingError as exc:
        return needle in str(exc)
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - a named FAIL beats a dead suite
        return False
    return False


def _printed(call) -> str:
    """What a call writes to stdout - for the reporting branches whose only observable effect IS
    the line they print. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]"""
    buffer = io.StringIO()
    with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buffer):
        call()
    return buffer.getvalue()


def _write(path: Path, text: str) -> Path:
    """Write a fixture file and hand its path straight back, for one-line self-test legs."""
    path.write_text(text, encoding="utf-8")
    return path


def _conf(path: Path, external: list[str], resources: dict[str, str]) -> Path:
    """Write a minimal `tauri.conf.json` fixture carrying just the two §3.3.1 bundle keys.

    Only `bundle` is written: the binder reads nothing else, and a fixture that mirrored the real
    config would drift from it. The REAL config is driven by its own leg, against the real row set.
    [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.31]
    """
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    return _write(
        path, json.dumps({"bundle": {"externalBin": external, "resources": resources}})
    )


def _gate_constant(name: str) -> int | None:
    """Read a named integer constant out of `scripts/check-build-matrix`.

    The G30 gate parses the same fat headers this file does. Freezing the pair mechanically is
    what keeps producer and gate from answering one question two ways - the drift is invisible
    otherwise, because each file's own suite is internally consistent. Reading (not importing)
    keeps this side of the L(-1) line: the gate is caged, its VALUE is not a secret.
    """
    gate = ROOT / "scripts" / "check-build-matrix"
    if not gate.is_file():
        return None
    for line in gate.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").splitlines():
        head, sep, tail = line.partition("=")
        if sep and head.strip() == name:
            try:
                return int(tail.split("#")[0].strip(), 0)
            except ValueError:
                return None
    return None


def _write_bytes(path: Path, payload: bytes) -> Path:
    """Write a binary fixture and hand its path back, for one-line self-test legs."""
    path.write_bytes(payload)
    return path


def _fixture_tree(base: Path) -> tuple[Path, Path]:
    """A repo-shaped root + a cache root holding one sidecar entry and one resource entry."""
    # RESOLVED for the same reason as _universal_fixture below: an expectation derived from an
    # unresolved base diverges from the tool's resolved spellings on a resolution-variant TEMP.
    base = base.resolve()
    root, cache = base / "repo", base / "cache"
    (root / BINARIES_REL).mkdir(parents=True)
    (root / RESOURCES_REL).mkdir(parents=True)
    sidecar_entry = cache / "ffmpeg-7.1-full_build-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu"
    sidecar_entry.mkdir(parents=True)
    (sidecar_entry / "ffmpeg").write_bytes(b"ELF-ish sidecar")
    (sidecar_entry / "ffprobe").write_bytes(b"ELF-ish sidecar 2")
    tree_entry = cache / "libreoffice-24.8-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu"
    (tree_entry / "program").mkdir(parents=True)
    (tree_entry / "program" / "soffice.bin").write_bytes(b"launcher")
    return root, cache


def _fat_bytes(cputypes: tuple[int, ...], *, magic: int = _FAT_MAGIC, count: int | None = None) -> bytes:
    """A synthetic fat Mach-O HEADER. The slices themselves are never parsed, so the arch table is
    the whole fixture - which is exactly what makes the assertion drivable off macOS."""
    width = 32 if magic == _FAT_MAGIC_64 else 20
    out = struct.pack(">II", magic, len(cputypes) if count is None else count)
    for cpu in cputypes:
        # UNSIGNED, matching the reader above and the G30 gate. Byte-identical for every real
        # cputype, so no fixture could ever have caught the mismatch - which is why the SIGNED
        # spelling survived here after the reader was aligned, until the dual review grepped the
        # file for the literal instead of trusting the claim. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29.1]
        out += struct.pack(">I", cpu) + b"\x00" * (width - 4)
    return out


_THIN_MACHO = b"\xcf\xfa\xed\xfe" + b"\x00" * 28
_FAT_BOTH = _fat_bytes((_CPU_TYPE_ARM64, _CPU_TYPE_X86_64))
_FAT_ARM_ONLY = _fat_bytes((_CPU_TYPE_ARM64,))


class _RelocationRunner:
    """A recording stand-in for the relocation toolchain, dispatching on the PROGRAM.

    ONE double for both halves - the tool that rewrites and the tool that reads back are the same
    toolchain, and a double that could only do one of them would let the read-back leg pass against
    a rewrite that never happened. The rewrite MUTATES its target (preserving the leading magic the
    format guard checks) so a leg can tell a real rewrite from a silent no-op; `corrupt`/`truncate`
    drive the two ways a real tool can wreck its input, and `readback` is what the read-back tool
    claims to see, which is how the silent-no-op refusal is driven. [Build-Session-Entscheidung:
    P4.30]
    """

    _WRITERS = ("install_name_tool", "patchelf")

    def __init__(self, *, fails: str | None = None, readback: str | None = None,
                 corrupt: bool = False, truncate: bool = False) -> None:
        self.calls: list[list[str]] = []
        self._fails = fails
        self._readback = readback
        self._corrupt = corrupt
        self._truncate = truncate

    def __call__(self, argv: list[str]):
        self.calls.append(list(argv))
        rc = 1 if self._fails == argv[0] else 0
        if argv[0] == "lipo":
            # The SAME seam carries the universal merge, so the one double has to answer for it -
            # otherwise the only way to drive stage_all's universal branch end to end would be two
            # doubles on one seam, which production does not have.
            if rc == 0:
                Path(argv[-1]).write_bytes(_FAT_BOTH)

            class _LipoResult:
                returncode = rc
                stdout = ""
                stderr = "tool refused" if rc else ""

            return _LipoResult()
        out = ""
        writing = argv[0] in self._WRITERS and "--print-rpath" not in argv
        if writing and rc == 0:
            target = Path(argv[-1])
            body = target.read_bytes()
            if self._truncate:
                target.write_bytes(b"")
            elif self._corrupt:
                target.write_bytes(b"ZZZZ" + body[4:])
            else:
                target.write_bytes(body + b"-relocated")
        elif not writing:
            # A dict lets a leg answer DIFFERENTLY per tool, which is the only way to tell a
            # read-back that matches each expectation to ITS tool from one that checks every
            # expectation against every output. A single string cannot discriminate that.
            if isinstance(self._readback, dict):
                out = self._readback.get(" ".join(argv[:2]), "")
            else:
                out = self._readback or ""

        class _Result:
            returncode = rc
            stdout = out
            stderr = "tool refused" if rc else ""

        return _Result()


class _LipoRunner:
    """A recording stand-in for `lipo`. Writes a fat header so the assertion has something real."""

    def __init__(self, *, fails: bool = False, output: bytes = _FAT_BOTH,
                 write_output: bool = True) -> None:
        self.calls: list[list[str]] = []
        self._fails = fails
        self._output = output
        self._write_output = write_output

    def __call__(self, argv: list[str]):
        self.calls.append(list(argv))
        if not self._fails and self._write_output:
            Path(argv[-1]).write_bytes(self._output)

        class _Result:
            returncode = 1 if self._fails else 0
            stdout = ""
            stderr = "fatal error: lipo: cannot open input file" if self._fails else ""

        return _Result()


def _modes_at_rename(run) -> list[int]:
    """Every source mode observed AT the `os.replace`, in call order.

    The mode legs assert only THAT a mode is set, which any ordering satisfies - so the whole
    reason `finalize` exists (the mode is in place before the file wears its final name) had no
    leg on any platform. Reading the mode inside the rename is the only place the ordering is
    observable at all: afterwards every ordering leaves an identical file.
    """
    seen: list[int] = []
    real_replace = os.replace

    def _watch(src, dst):
        seen.append(os.stat(src).st_mode)
        return real_replace(src, dst)

    os.replace = _watch
    try:
        run()
    finally:
        os.replace = real_replace
    return seen


def _universal_fixture(base: Path) -> tuple[Path, Path]:
    """A repo root + a cache holding BOTH per-arch slices of one sidecar and one resource tree."""
    # RESOLVED once, deliberately: the tool hands `lipo` RESOLVED member paths (`_member_path`'s
    # resolve-then-contain discipline), so an exact-argv expectation derived from an UNRESOLVED
    # base diverges wherever the temp dir's spelling is not resolution-invariant - macOS
    # (/var -> /private/var) and the CI Windows runner (an 8.3 RUNNER~1 component) both diverge,
    # which is how the first Lane-A run reddened the exact-argv leg that was green on this dev
    # host and ubuntu (the validate-on-CI-runtime class; reproduced locally via a junction TEMP).
    # Resolving the BASE makes every expectation derived from these paths agree with the tool's
    # spelling BY CONSTRUCTION. On resolution-invariant hosts resolve() is the identity; on a
    # VARIANT host the pre-fix leg asserted the INVERSE of the tool's contract - it would have
    # gone green had _member_path stopped resolving - so the change STRENGTHENS the assertion
    # exactly where it was inverted. The old expectation encoded the host accident "temp is
    # resolution-invariant"; the tool's resolution is the deliberate containment behaviour, so
    # the expectation follows the tool, never the reverse.
    # [Test-Change: P4.29 - old-obsolete+new-correct, §6.1.3]
    base = base.resolve()
    root, cache = base / "repo", base / "cache"
    (root / BINARIES_REL).mkdir(parents=True)
    (root / RESOURCES_REL).mkdir(parents=True)
    for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
        entry = cache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{slice_triple}"
        entry.mkdir(parents=True)
        (entry / "ffmpeg").write_bytes(_THIN_MACHO + slice_triple.encode())
        tree = cache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{slice_triple}"
        (tree / "program").mkdir(parents=True)
        (tree / "program" / "soffice.bin").write_bytes(_THIN_MACHO + slice_triple.encode())
        (tree / "program" / "shared.rdb").write_bytes(b"arch-independent data")
    return root, cache


def _plant_link(link: Path, target: Path, *, directory: bool) -> str | None:
    """Plant a link at `link` pointing at `target`; returns its shape, or None if the host can't.

    Two shapes, and they are NOT interchangeable. A POSIX symlink is planted relatively, because
    an absolute one trips a different guard than the one under test. Where the host refuses a
    symlink outright - the DEFAULT Windows configuration, which lacks
    SeCreateSymbolicLinkPrivilege - a directory JUNCTION stands in: the shape `copytree`
    MATERIALISES, and the reason the tree guard runs on the source at all. So each host proves the
    half of the rule it is able to express, and only a host that can express NEITHER skips.
    """
    try:
        link.symlink_to(os.path.relpath(target, link.parent), target_is_directory=directory)
        return "symlink"
    except (OSError, NotImplementedError):
        pass
    if not directory or os.name != "nt":
        return None
    made = subprocess.run(
        ["cmd", "/c", "mklink", "/J", str(link), str(target)], capture_output=True, check=False
    )
    return "junction" if made.returncode == 0 else None


_UNIVERSAL_ROWS = (
    EngineStaging("ffmpeg", StagingKind.SIDECAR, "ffmpeg"),
    EngineStaging("libreoffice", StagingKind.RESOURCE_TREE, "libreoffice", member="program"),
)

_LINUX = "x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu"
_WIN = "x86_64-pc-windows-msvc"
_FIXTURE_ROWS = (
    EngineStaging("ffmpeg", StagingKind.SIDECAR, "ffmpeg"),
    EngineStaging("ffmpeg", StagingKind.SIDECAR, "ffprobe"),
    EngineStaging("libreoffice", StagingKind.RESOURCE_TREE, "libreoffice"),
)


def selftest() -> int:
    # (1) §3.4.5 per-OS layout: the suffix keys off the triple, never off the host.
    _record("exe suffix: windows triple -> .exe", exe_suffix(_WIN) == ".exe")
    _record(
        "exe suffix: macOS/Linux triples -> none",
        exe_suffix(_LINUX) == "" and exe_suffix("aarch64-apple-darwin") == "",
    )
    _record(
        "externalBin naming: <name>-<triple>[.exe]",
        sidecar_filename("ffmpeg", _WIN) == "ffmpeg-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.exe"
        and sidecar_filename("ffmpeg", _LINUX) == "ffmpeg-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu",
    )

    # (2) target resolution, incl. the universal target this box makes resolvable.
    _record("target: explicit v1 triple accepted", resolve_target(_LINUX) == _LINUX)
    # [Test-Change: P4.29 - old-obsolete+new-correct, §6.1.3] the leg asserted that
    # `universal-apple-darwin` is REFUSED, with a message naming P4.29 as the box that would make
    # it work. This IS that box: the refusal is the obsolete expectation, and the new one - the
    # target resolves to itself - is verified below by staging through it end to end.
    _record("target: universal-apple-darwin accepted (P4.29)", resolve_target(UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE) == UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE)
    # (2a) the universal merge: the fat parse, the assertion, the lipo seam, the dual sourcing.
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        ubase = Path(tmp).resolve()  # the exact-argv leg below needs the tool's spelling
        _record(
            "fat parse: a two-slice header reports both cputypes",
            fat_slice_cputypes(_write_bytes(ubase / "fat", _FAT_BOTH))
            == (_CPU_TYPE_ARM64, _CPU_TYPE_X86_64),
        )
        _record(
            "fat parse: a 64-bit fat header is read at its own arch width",
            fat_slice_cputypes(
                _write_bytes(ubase / "fat64",
                             _fat_bytes((_CPU_TYPE_ARM64, _CPU_TYPE_X86_64), magic=_FAT_MAGIC_64))
            )
            == (_CPU_TYPE_ARM64, _CPU_TYPE_X86_64),
        )
        _record(
            "fat parse: a THIN Mach-O is not fat",
            fat_slice_cputypes(_write_bytes(ubase / "thin", _THIN_MACHO)) == (),
        )
        _record(
            "fat parse: a non-Mach-O is not fat",
            fat_slice_cputypes(_write_bytes(ubase / "data", b"just some bytes")) == (),
        )
        _record(
            "fat parse: the cputype constants are read from a LITERAL header, not from themselves",
            # Every other fixture is generated FROM these constants, so a wrong value would be
            # invisible - and a wrong cputype refuses every genuine universal artifact, on the one
            # platform none of this can be exercised locally.
            fat_slice_cputypes(
                _write_bytes(
                    ubase / "literal",
                    bytes.fromhex("cafebabe00000002" + "0100000c" + "00" * 16
                                  + "01000007" + "00" * 16),
                )
            )
            == (_CPU_TYPE_ARM64, _CPU_TYPE_X86_64),
        )
        _record(
            "fat parse: the arch bound AGREES with the G30 gate that parses the same bytes",
            # Producer and gate answer one question about one file; two values would mean one
            # accepts what the other refuses, and each file's own suite stays internally
            # consistent either way, so the drift is invisible without this bind. Frozen against
            # the gate's own source, for EVERY such constant rather than one at a time: the fat64
            # magic was unpinned and undetectable, because the fixtures are generated FROM it.
            all(
                _gate_constant(there) == globals()[here]
                for there, here in _GATE_PINNED_CONSTANTS.items()
            )
            # ...and the mapping covers every integer constant this module defines under the
            # three fat-header PREFIXES, so a new magic, cputype or bound cannot arrive unpinned.
            # Prefix-bounded, not shape-complete: a constant named outside them is still invisible
            # here, which is the honest limit of a rule written over names.
            and set(_GATE_PINNED_CONSTANTS.values())
            == {
                name
                for name, value in globals().items()
                # The INTEGER header constants: `_CPU_TYPE_NAMES` shares the prefix but is the
                # diagnostic spelling table, which the gate homes under a name of its own.
                if isinstance(value, int)
                and name.startswith(("_FAT_MAGIC", "_CPU_TYPE", "_MAX_FAT"))
            },
        )
        _record(
            "fat parse: a declared count the table does not carry is refused",
            fat_slice_cputypes(
                _write_bytes(ubase / "liar", _fat_bytes((_CPU_TYPE_ARM64,), count=2))
            )
            == (),
        )
        _record(
            "fat parse: a header shorter than 8 bytes is refused",
            fat_slice_cputypes(_write_bytes(ubase / "stub", b"\xca\xfe")) == (),
        )
        _record(
            "fat parse: a non-fat MAGIC with a plausible header is still not fat",
            # `b"just some bytes"` could not discriminate this: its header yields an absurd count
            # that the BOUND rejects anyway, so the magic check itself went unasserted. An ELF
            # magic with a small, well-formed arch table isolates it.
            fat_slice_cputypes(
                _write_bytes(ubase / "elf",
                             struct.pack(">II", 0x7F454C46, 2) + b"\x00" * 40)
            )
            == (),
        )
        _record(
            "fat parse: an absurd arch count is refused, not trusted",
            # A Java `.class` opens with the same 0xCAFEBABE. The table is written in FULL, so the
            # truncation check cannot mask the bound: without it this parses as `_MAX_FAT_ARCH + 1` slices.
            fat_slice_cputypes(
                _write_bytes(
                    ubase / "classfile",
                    _fat_bytes(tuple([_CPU_TYPE_ARM64] * (_MAX_FAT_ARCH + 1))),
                )
            )
            == (),
        )
        _record(
            "fat parse: a truncated arch table is refused",
            fat_slice_cputypes(_write_bytes(ubase / "cut", _FAT_BOTH[:-8])) == (),
        )
        _record(
            "G30 assertion: a both-slice fat binary passes",
            assert_universal_fat(_write_bytes(ubase / "ok", _FAT_BOTH), "probe") is None,
        )
        _record(
            "G30 assertion: a THIN binary wearing the universal name is refused",
            _raises(
                lambda: assert_universal_fat(_write_bytes(ubase / "thin2", _THIN_MACHO), "probe"),
                "is not a fat Mach-O",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "G30 assertion: a fat binary missing a slice names WHICH one",
            _raises(
                lambda: assert_universal_fat(
                    _write_bytes(ubase / "arm", _FAT_ARM_ONLY), "probe"
                ),
                "missing [x86_64]",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "is_macho: thin and fat are Mach-O, data is not",
            is_macho(ubase / "thin")
            and is_macho(ubase / "fat")
            and not is_macho(ubase / "data"),
        )
        runner = _LipoRunner()
        merge_universal_binary([ubase / "thin", ubase / "thin2"], ubase / "merged", "probe",
                               runner=runner)
        _record(
            "lipo: driven as an argv LIST, `-create <slices> -output <staging sibling>`",
            runner.calls == [["lipo", "-create", str(ubase / "thin"), str(ubase / "thin2"),
                              "-output", str(ubase / ".merged.merging")]],
        )
        _record(
            "lipo: the merge is ATOMIC - the staging sibling never survives",
            (ubase / "merged").is_file() and not (ubase / ".merged.merging").exists(),
        )
        _record(
            "lipo: a merge REFUSED for thinness leaves no file under the real name",
            _raises(
                lambda: merge_universal_binary([ubase / "thin"], ubase / "refused", "probe",
                                               runner=_LipoRunner(output=_THIN_MACHO)),
                "is not a fat Mach-O",
            )
            and not (ubase / "refused").exists()
            and not (ubase / ".refused.merging").exists(),
        )
        _record(
            "lipo: a failing merge is a clear refusal, not a silent miss",
            _raises(
                lambda: merge_universal_binary([ubase / "thin"], ubase / "x", "probe",
                                               runner=_LipoRunner(fails=True)),
                "`lipo -create` failed",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "run_tool passes an argv LIST - a path with a space stays ONE argument",
            # Every leg above drives the injected runner, so the one place a real process is
            # spawned would otherwise be unexercised: switching it to a joined shell line passed
            # the whole suite.
            (lambda probe: probe.returncode == 0 and probe.stdout.strip() == "['one two']")(
                run_tool([sys.executable, "-c",
                          "import sys; sys.stdout.write(repr(sys.argv[1:]))", "one two"])
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "lipo: success with no output file is still a refusal",
            _raises(
                lambda: merge_universal_binary([ubase / "thin"], ubase / "y", "probe",
                                               runner=_LipoRunner(write_output=False)),
                "wrote no y",
            ),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        _record(
            "dual sourcing: both per-arch entries are found for one row",
            sorted(_slice_entries(ucache, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[0])) == sorted(UNIVERSAL_SLICES),
        )
        _record(
            "dual sourcing: an engine absent from BOTH slices is absent, not an error",
            _slice_entries(ucache, EngineStaging("nope", StagingKind.SIDECAR, "nope")) is None,
        )
        shutil.rmtree(ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}")
        _record(
            "dual sourcing: ONE slice present is a refusal naming the missing one and P4.29.1",
            _raises(
                lambda: _slice_entries(ucache, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[0]),
                "P4.29.1",
            ),
        )
        # The refusal names the REMEDY, and which remedy it is depends on WHICH slice is gone: on
        # the arm64 universal runner a missing x86_64 slice is §6.1.3's cross fallback while a
        # missing aarch64 slice is an ordinary native compile. `missing` is symmetric over
        # UNIVERSAL_SLICES, so a leg driving only one arm cannot see the other arm's wording go
        # wrong - the arm-blind shape this file's own `_raises` contract exists to forbid. TWO
        # arms, TWO legs, distinct needles. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.29.1]
        _record(
            "dual sourcing: the missing-x86_64 arm names the CROSS remedy and its exact --target",
            _raises(
                lambda: _slice_entries(ucache, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[0]),
                f"--target {UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}",
            )
            and _raises(lambda: _slice_entries(ucache, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[0]), "a cross build"),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        _, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        # The OTHER arm, on its own fixture: removing the aarch64 entry instead. Driven through a
        # second fixture rather than by deleting both in turn, because an entry removed from the
        # first one cannot be put back.
        shutil.rmtree(ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]}")
        _record(
            "dual sourcing: the missing-aarch64 arm names a NATIVE compile, not a cross build",
            _raises(
                lambda: _slice_entries(ucache, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[0]),
                f"--target {UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]}",
            )
            and _raises(lambda: _slice_entries(ucache, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[0]), "a native build"),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        merge_runner = _LipoRunner()
        results = stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS,
                            runner=merge_runner)
        merged = uroot / BINARIES_REL / f"ffmpeg-{UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE}"
        _record(
            "universal staging: the sidecar lands under the universal externalBin name",
            merged.is_file() and len([r for r in results if r.dest is not None]) == 2,
        )
        _record(
            "universal staging: lipo was handed BOTH slices, in UNIVERSAL_SLICES order",
            # The exact argv, not a substring search: the ORDER decides which slice `lipo` reads
            # first, and a single-slice call would still satisfy any "contains arm64" test.
            merge_runner.calls[0]
            == [
                "lipo",
                "-create",
                str(ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]}" / "ffmpeg"),
                str(ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}" / "ffmpeg"),
                "-output",
                # the STAGING sibling: the merge is atomic, so `lipo` never writes the real name.
                str(merged.with_name(f".{merged.name}.merging")),
            ],
        )
        _record(
            "universal staging: the merged sidecar is asserted fat with BOTH slices",
            fat_slice_cputypes(merged) == (_CPU_TYPE_ARM64, _CPU_TYPE_X86_64),
        )
        _record(
            "universal staging: the merged sidecar keeps its exec bit",
            bool(merged.stat().st_mode & stat.S_IXUSR) if os.name != "nt" else True,
        )
        _record(
            "universal staging: the resource tree's Mach-O member is lipo'd, its data copied",
            (uroot / RESOURCES_REL / "libreoffice" / "soffice.bin").is_file()
            and (uroot / RESOURCES_REL / "libreoffice" / "shared.rdb").read_bytes()
            == b"arch-independent data",
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        (ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}" / "ffmpeg").unlink()
        _record(
            "universal staging: a member missing from ONE slice's entry is named, not a lipo error",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[:1],
                                  runner=_LipoRunner()),
                f"is missing from the restored {UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]} cache entry",
            ),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        shutil.rmtree(ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}" / "program")
        _record(
            "universal staging: a resource MEMBER that is not a directory is named",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:],
                                  runner=_LipoRunner()),
                "stages a DIRECTORY",
            ),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        stale = uroot / RESOURCES_REL / "libreoffice"
        stale.mkdir(parents=True)
        (stale / "left-over.rdb").write_bytes(b"from a previous run")
        stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:], runner=_LipoRunner())
        _record(
            "universal staging: the resource destination is CLEARED, not merged into",
            not (stale / "left-over.rdb").exists() and (stale / "shared.rdb").is_file(),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
            (ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{slice_triple}" / "program" / "plugins").mkdir()
        stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:], runner=_LipoRunner())
        _record(
            "universal staging: a directory both slices carry SURVIVES the merge",
            (uroot / RESOURCES_REL / "libreoffice" / "plugins").is_dir(),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        already = _FAT_BOTH
        for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
            (ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{slice_triple}" / "program" / "fat.dylib").write_bytes(already)
        copy_runner = _LipoRunner()
        stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:], runner=copy_runner)
        _record(
            "universal staging: an ALREADY-fat identical member is copied, never re-merged",
            # `lipo -create` refuses overlapping architectures, so handing it two copies of one
            # fat binary would fail a correct input.
            (uroot / RESOURCES_REL / "libreoffice" / "fat.dylib").read_bytes() == already
            and not any("fat.dylib" in " ".join(call) for call in copy_runner.calls),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        (ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}").rename(
            ucache / f"ffmpeg-9.9-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}"
        )
        _record(
            "universal staging: the two slices must pin the SAME version",
            # Nothing downstream can see this: a fat header carries no source version and the
            # SBOM lists one. This is the only point where both version strings are in hand.
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[:1],
                                  runner=_LipoRunner()),
                "pin DIFFERENT versions",
            ),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        (ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]}" / "program" / "either").mkdir()
        (ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}" / "program" / "either").write_bytes(b"file")
        _record(
            "universal staging: a DIRECTORY in one slice and a file in the other is refused",
            # Portable, unlike the symlink mismatches: no privilege is needed to disagree about
            # whether a path is a directory.
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:],
                                  runner=_LipoRunner()),
                "is a directory in one slice and a file in the other",
            ),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        (ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]}" / "program" / "maybe.dylib").write_bytes(_THIN_MACHO)
        (ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}" / "program" / "maybe.dylib").write_bytes(b"not a mach-o")
        _record(
            "universal staging: a Mach-O in ONE slice only is a divergence, not a merge",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:],
                                  runner=_LipoRunner()),
                # The message names WHICH slice carries the Mach-O, and the fixture puts it in
                # the FIRST - so a swapped pair of roots would name the wrong one and still raise.
                # A diagnostic that points at the innocent slice is worse than none.
                f"is a Mach-O in {UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]} and not in the other",
            ),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        one = ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]}" / "program"
        two = ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}" / "program"
        try:
            (one / "opt").symlink_to("shared.rdb")
            _mixed_linkable = True
        except OSError:
            _mixed_linkable = False
        if _mixed_linkable:
            (two / "opt").write_bytes(b"arch-independent data")
            _record(
                "universal staging: a symlink in one slice and a FILE in the other is refused",
                # Distinct from the two target-disagreement legs: both of those symlink BOTH
                # sides, so this mixed-TYPE branch was unexercised on every platform.
                _raises(
                    lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:],
                                      runner=_LipoRunner()),
                    "a real file in the other slice",
                ),
            )
        else:
            _record("universal staging: symlink-vs-file leg (skipped - host cannot create symlinks)", True)

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
            (ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{slice_triple}" / "program" / "same.dylib").write_bytes(_THIN_MACHO)
        _record(
            "universal staging: two IDENTICAL thin Mach-Os are refused, not copied through",
            # Byte-identity is not evidence of fatness. This is the path the already-fat member
            # takes, and it must not become a way past the G30 net - which cannot see a tree
            # member at all, since it finds universal artifacts by NAME.
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:],
                                  runner=_LipoRunner()),
                "is not a fat Mach-O",
            ),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
            member = ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{slice_triple}" / "program" / "soffice.bin"
            if os.name != "nt":
                _set_exec_bit(member)
        stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:], runner=_LipoRunner())
        staged_member = uroot / RESOURCES_REL / "libreoffice" / "soffice.bin"
        _record(
            "universal staging: a MERGED tree member keeps the mode its slice had",
            # `lipo -create` writes its own mode, which is why the sidecar path sets the bit
            # explicitly. §3.3.1 stages LibreOffice's launcher inside the resources tree, so a
            # dropped +x here fails every conversion on the universal build alone.
            bool(staged_member.stat().st_mode & stat.S_IXUSR) if os.name != "nt" else True,
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        first = ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]}" / "program"
        second = ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}" / "program"
        # A sibling subtree in BOTH slices, so the link has something contained and RELATIVE to
        # point at. Pointing it at its own parent - the obvious shape - planted an ABSOLUTE link
        # (pathlib hands the target straight to `os.symlink`), which `assert_no_escaping_links`
        # refuses on an arm of its own BEFORE the merge is entered: the leg was green here only
        # because this host skips it, and would have recorded a FAIL on every POSIX runner. A
        # symlink is the whole point of this leg, so there is no junction fallback - a junction is
        # not a symlink and legitimately takes the directory branch.
        for program in (first, second):
            (program / "other").mkdir()
            (program / "other" / "member.rdb").write_bytes(b"arch-independent data")
        (first / "sub").mkdir()
        try:
            (second / "sub").symlink_to("other", target_is_directory=True)
            _dirlink = True
        except OSError:
            _dirlink = False
        if _dirlink:
            _record(
                "universal staging: a real DIR against a symlink-to-dir is refused, not flattened",
                # `is_dir()` follows the link, so checking only the primary side would take the
                # directory branch and publish an empty dir, dropping the other slice's layout.
                _raises(
                    lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:],
                                      runner=_LipoRunner()),
                    # Discriminated on the OTHER side's real type: the symlink-vs-FILE leg reaches
                    # this same guard, so a shared message would let either leg pass on the
                    # other's input.
                    "a real directory in the other slice",
                ),
            )
        else:
            _record("universal staging: dir-vs-dirlink leg (skipped - host cannot create symlinks)", True)

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
            (ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{slice_triple}" / "ffmpeg").write_bytes(_FAT_BOTH)
        already_runner = _LipoRunner()
        stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[:1], runner=already_runner)
        _record(
            "universal staging: an upstream that already ships universal is COPIED, not re-merged",
            # `lipo -create` refuses two copies of one fat binary, so merging would fail a
            # perfectly good input with a raw tool error.
            already_runner.calls == []
            and (uroot / BINARIES_REL / f"ffmpeg-{UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE}").read_bytes() == _FAT_BOTH
            # ...and published through the same staging sibling the merge uses, leaving none.
            and not (uroot / BINARIES_REL
                     / f".ffmpeg-{UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE}.merging").exists()
            # ...carrying the exec bit, which only the MERGE path had a leg for: a copied sidecar
            # inherits whatever mode the cache entry had, and §3.4.5/G37 surfaces a dropped +x as
            # a misleading startup "integrity failed" rather than as the staging fault it is.
            and (bool((uroot / BINARIES_REL / f"ffmpeg-{UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE}")
                      .stat().st_mode & stat.S_IXUSR) if os.name != "nt" else True),
        )
        # Setup on its OWN statement: chained into the lambda with `and`, the assertion hung off
        # the truthiness of a list comprehension - the shape that silently skips the very call it
        # is meant to guard.
        for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
            (ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{slice_triple}" / "ffmpeg").write_bytes(_FAT_ARM_ONLY)
        _record(
            "universal staging: an already-universal sidecar is still asserted fat",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[:1],
                                  runner=_LipoRunner()),
                "missing [x86_64]",
            ),
        )
        for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
            (ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{slice_triple}" / "ffmpeg").write_bytes(_FAT_BOTH)
        previous = b"the previous run's universal sidecar"
        dest_path = uroot / BINARIES_REL / f"ffmpeg-{UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE}"
        dest_path.write_bytes(previous)
        _real_copy2 = shutil.copy2

        def _copy_then_fail(src, dst):
            """Land the bytes, THEN fail - the one ordering a residue check cannot distinguish."""
            _real_copy2(src, dst)
            raise OSError("simulated failure after the bytes landed")

        shutil.copy2 = _copy_then_fail
        try:
            stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[:1], runner=_LipoRunner())
        except OSError:
            raised = True
        else:
            raised = False
        finally:
            shutil.copy2 = _real_copy2
        _record(
            "universal staging: a failure AFTER the bytes land leaves no TORN universal sidecar",
            # The atomicity of the already-universal path, which the residue leg above cannot
            # see: a direct `copy2` into the destination leaves no `.merging` residue either, so
            # both shapes look identical unless the copy is made to fail once the bytes are down.
            # What survives such a run is a half-written file wearing the universal name, and the
            # NEXT run - one whose engine is simply absent from the cache - exits 0 with it still
            # in place, straight into `tauri build`. Same reason the merge stages and replaces.
            raised
            and dest_path.read_bytes() == previous
            and not (uroot / BINARIES_REL / f".ffmpeg-{UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE}.merging").exists(),
        )
        _real_replace = os.replace

        def _fail_at_the_publish(*_args):
            """Fail AT the rename - the step a mid-copy failure never reaches."""
            raise OSError("simulated failure at the publish")

        os.replace = _fail_at_the_publish
        try:
            stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[:1], runner=_LipoRunner())
        except OSError:
            published_by_rename = True
        else:
            published_by_rename = False
        finally:
            os.replace = _real_replace
        _record(
            "universal staging: the already-universal sidecar is PUBLISHED by a rename",
            # The leg above fails mid-COPY and therefore never reaches the publish, so swapping
            # `os.replace` for a second `copy2` survives it entirely - and a copied publish is
            # exactly the window the staging sibling exists to close. Two failure points, because
            # one injection cannot observe both steps.
            published_by_rename
            and dest_path.read_bytes() == previous
            and not (uroot / BINARIES_REL / f".ffmpeg-{UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE}.merging").exists(),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
            (ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{slice_triple}" / "ffmpeg").write_bytes(_THIN_MACHO)
        _record(
            "universal --check refuses a THIN already-universal SIDECAR, as the real run does",
            # The sidecar twin of the tree's --check depth, and the sibling the tree fix left
            # open: identical bytes in both slices take the copy path, whose fatness verdict is a
            # judgement on the restored cache rather than a consequence of writing. `ci.yml`'s
            # check pass runs `--check` for the runner's HOST triple, so this branch has no live
            # CI consumer until P10.10 wires the universal one - which is exactly why the
            # divergence had to be closed now rather than discovered then, on the artifact G30
            # looks for BY that universal name.
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[:1],
                                  write=False, runner=_LipoRunner()),
                "is not a fat Mach-O",
            )
            and not (uroot / BINARIES_REL / f"ffmpeg-{UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE}").exists(),
        )
        # Exactly ONE Mach-O: the shape this branch is NAMED for, and the one its diagnostic
        # SPELLS per slice. Two non-Mach-Os make that spelling symmetric, so the pair of legs
        # below covers both the refusal and the naming - a diagnostic that points at the innocent
        # slice is the sibling defect the tree path's message pin just closed.
        (ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]}" / "ffmpeg").write_bytes(_THIN_MACHO)
        (ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}" / "ffmpeg").write_bytes(b"a wrapper script")
        _record(
            "universal staging: ONE Mach-O sidecar slice against a non-Mach-O is refused BY SLICE",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[:1],
                                  runner=_LipoRunner()),
                f"{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]}=Mach-O, {UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}=not a Mach-O",
            ),
        )
        for slice_triple, payload in zip(UNIVERSAL_SLICES, (b"a wrapper script", b"a different one")):
            (ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{slice_triple}" / "ffmpeg").write_bytes(payload)
        _record(
            "universal staging: two DIFFERING sidecar slices that are not both Mach-O are NAMED",
            # Asserted in BOTH modes because they fail for two different reasons: the real run
            # would have died on a raw `lipo` error naming neither row nor slice, and --check,
            # which never runs `lipo`, accepted the pair outright.
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[:1],
                                  runner=_LipoRunner()),
                "not both Mach-O",
            )
            and _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[:1],
                                  write=False, runner=_LipoRunner()),
                "not both Mach-O",
            ),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        # An entry keyed to the SECOND slice only, claimed by no row: visible only if the scan
        # unions both slice triples. Driven through `main` with a patched row set, the way the
        # per-triple form is already covered.
        (ucache / f"libheif-1.0-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}").mkdir()
        # `main()` binds the row set against the tree's `tauri.conf.json` since P4.31, so a fixture
        # root driven through `main` needs one - declared BOUND to `_UNIVERSAL_ROWS`, since this
        # leg is about the unclaimed-entry scan and not about the binder.
        # [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.31]
        _conf(
            uroot / TAURI_CONF_REL,
            [f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}ffmpeg"],
            {f"{BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX}libreoffice/": "engines/libreoffice/"},
        )
        _saved_rows = STAGED_ENGINES
        buffer = io.StringIO()
        try:
            globals()["STAGED_ENGINES"] = _UNIVERSAL_ROWS
            with contextlib.redirect_stdout(buffer):
                main(["--root", str(uroot), "--cache-root", str(ucache), "--target",
                      UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, "--check", "-v"])
        finally:
            globals()["STAGED_ENGINES"] = _saved_rows
        _record(
            "universal --check reports an UNCLAIMED entry from either slice triple",
            # A `-universal-apple-darwin`-suffix scan would always match nothing - the cache is
            # keyed per real triple - silencing the "or a MISSING staging row" half of the signal
            # on exactly the run that reads two slices.
            f"libheif-1.0-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}" in buffer.getvalue(),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        results = stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[:1],
                            runner=_LipoRunner())
        _record(
            "universal staging: the result records the PRIMARY slice's entry",
            results[0].entry == ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]}",
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        _record(
            "universal staging: a lipo that emits a THIN binary is refused, not shipped",
            # The G30 case exactly: `lipo` succeeded, the file exists and wears the universal
            # name, and it carries one architecture. Tauri would bundle it without complaint.
            # SIDECAR-only rows: `_UNIVERSAL_ROWS` also carries a resource tree, whose merge
            # asserts fatness too - so with the sidecar's own assertion deleted the leg would
            # still raise, from the wrong place.
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[:1],
                                  runner=_LipoRunner(output=_THIN_MACHO)),
                "is not a fat Mach-O",
            ),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        _record(
            "universal staging: a THIN tree MEMBER is refused too - G30 cannot see it by name",
            # The complement of the sidecar leg: `check-build-matrix` finds universal artifacts by
            # NAME, and a merged tree member is called `soffice.bin`. This assertion is the only
            # net for it, so it needs its own leg on the TREE-only row set.
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:],
                                  runner=_LipoRunner(output=_THIN_MACHO)),
                "resource tree 'libreoffice': soffice.bin",
            ),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        shutil.copytree(ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]}",
                        ucache / f"ffmpeg-9.9-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]}")
        _record(
            "universal staging: two pinned VERSIONS of one engine are refused per slice",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS,
                                  runner=_LipoRunner()),
                "exactly one pinned version may be restored per engine",
            ),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        # A DIFFERENT mechanism from the version clash above: `cache_entry_matches` anchors on the
        # two ends, so `libvips-tools-1.0-<slice>` parses as belonging to BOTH `libvips` and
        # `libvips-tools`. That check runs per SLICE triple, and nothing drove it there.
        prefix_rows = (
            EngineStaging("libvips", StagingKind.SIDECAR, "libvips"),
            EngineStaging("libvips-tools", StagingKind.SIDECAR, "libvips-tools"),
        )
        # The SECOND slice only: planted in both, restricting the scan to `UNIVERSAL_SLICES[:1]`
        # still finds it, so the leg could not tell a per-slice scan from a primary-only one - the
        # same reasoning the escaping-link leg states, applied here too.
        entry = ucache / f"libvips-tools-1.0-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}"
        entry.mkdir(parents=True)
        (entry / "libvips-tools").write_bytes(_THIN_MACHO)
        _record(
            "universal staging: an entry TWO declared engines claim is refused, per slice",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, prefix_rows,
                                  runner=_LipoRunner()),
                "must identify exactly one engine",
            ),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        program = ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]}" / "program"
        other = ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}" / "program"
        try:
            (program / "link").symlink_to("soffice.bin")
            (other / "link").symlink_to("shared.rdb")
            _linkable_u = True
        except OSError:
            _linkable_u = False
        if _linkable_u:
            _record(
                "universal staging: the two slices pointing one symlink elsewhere is refused",
                _raises(
                    lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS,
                                      runner=_LipoRunner()),
                    "different targets",
                ),
            )
            (other / "link").unlink()
            (other / "link").symlink_to("soffice.bin")
            stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS, runner=_LipoRunner())
            staged_link = uroot / RESOURCES_REL / "libreoffice" / "link"
            _record(
                "universal staging: a symlink agreeing across slices is PRESERVED, not merged",
                staged_link.is_symlink() and os.readlink(staged_link) == "soffice.bin",
            )
        else:
            _record("universal staging: symlink-disagreement leg (skipped - host cannot create symlinks)", True)
            _record("universal staging: symlink-preserve leg (skipped - host cannot create symlinks)", True)

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        outside = Path(tmp) / "outside-the-entry"
        outside.mkdir()
        (outside / "secret.txt").write_bytes(b"out-of-entry sentinel")
        # The SECOND slice deliberately: `stage_universal` walks BOTH restored trees, so a guard
        # placed on the primary alone would pass every leg written against the first while the
        # other slice walked straight out of its cache entry.
        escape = ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[1]}" / "program" / "escape.link"
        if _plant_link(escape, outside, directory=True):
            _record(
                "universal staging: an escaping link in EITHER slice is refused, on --check too",
                # A call site of its own: the per-triple twin in stage_resource_tree is covered by
                # the (5d) legs, and nothing bound the universal path to the same refusal - a
                # merge that dropped it would pull out-of-entry content into the bundle on exactly
                # the target that has no per-arch build to compare against (§0.11 T3a / G37b).
                _raises(
                    lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:],
                                      runner=_LipoRunner()),
                    "is a link resolving outside the staged tree",
                )
                and _raises(
                    lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:],
                                      write=False, runner=_LipoRunner()),
                    "is a link resolving outside the staged tree",
                ),
            )
        else:
            _record(
                "universal staging: escaping-link leg (skipped - host cannot create any link)",
                True,
            )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        for slice_triple, payload in zip(UNIVERSAL_SLICES, (b"one", b"two")):
            (ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{slice_triple}" / "program" / "diverges.rdb").write_bytes(payload)
        _record(
            "universal --check reaches member CONTENT, not only the two path SETS",
            # A --check that returned at the path-set diff validated strictly LESS than the real
            # run, and a dry run greener than the wet one is the one thing a check mode must never
            # be: CI would sign off a tree `stage-engines` then refuses at package time.
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:],
                                  write=False, runner=_LipoRunner()),
                "no merge rule for it",
            ),
        )
        for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
            (ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{slice_triple}" / "program" / "diverges.rdb").write_bytes(b"agreed")
            (ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{slice_triple}" / "program" / "thin.dylib").write_bytes(_THIN_MACHO)
        _record(
            "universal --check refuses an identical THIN Mach-O, exactly as the real run does",
            # The byte-identity branch's fatness assertion is a VERDICT on the restored cache, not
            # a consequence of writing, so it has to hold in both modes.
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:],
                                  write=False, runner=_LipoRunner()),
                "is not a fat Mach-O",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "universal --check still writes NOTHING while validating that deeply",
            # The other direction of the same change: deepening --check must not have turned it
            # into a run that mutates src-tauri/ behind a flag whose whole promise is that it does
            # not.
            not (uroot / RESOURCES_REL / "libreoffice").exists(),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        shapes = set()
        for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
            program = ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{slice_triple}" / "program"
            # `aaa` sorts BEFORE `zlink`, so the link's target is already staged when the link is
            # reached: a descent would then write THROUGH it instead of failing on a dangling
            # parent, which is what makes the two outcomes differ by a countable merge rather than
            # by an incidental OSError.
            (program / "aaa").mkdir()
            (program / "aaa" / "lib.dylib").write_bytes(_THIN_MACHO + slice_triple.encode())
            shapes.add(_plant_link(program / "zlink", program / "aaa", directory=True))
        if shapes == {"symlink"} or shapes == {"junction"}:
            walk_runner = _LipoRunner()
            stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:], runner=walk_runner)
            staged = uroot / RESOURCES_REL / "libreoffice"
            # Counted UNDER the link rather than in total: the fixture merges its own members too,
            # so a bare call count would pin an unrelated number and drift with the fixture.
            under_link = [
                call[-1] for call in walk_runner.calls if "zlink" in Path(call[-1]).parts
            ]
            _record(
                "universal staging: the tree walk descends exactly as `copytree` does",
                # The rule is spelled in `relatives`, not inherited from `rglob`'s stdlib default:
                # a SYMLINK is preserved and never entered (so `aaa/lib.dylib` is merged ONCE),
                # a JUNCTION is entered and materialised (so `zlink/lib.dylib` is a real merged
                # file). Either half failing means the universal bundle differs from the three
                # per-arch ones for a reason no downstream gate could name.
                (not under_link and (staged / "zlink").is_symlink())
                if shapes == {"symlink"}
                else (len(under_link) == 1 and (staged / "zlink" / "lib.dylib").is_file()),
            )
        else:
            _record(
                "universal staging: tree-walk descent leg (skipped - host cannot create any link)",
                True,
            )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        # READ-ONLY, not the exec bit: Windows does not carry `S_IXUSR` on a file without an
        # executable extension, so an exec-bit assertion is inert on this host - and the ordering
        # is exactly the property that had no leg on ANY platform. Windows DOES honour the write
        # bit, so `copystat` from a read-only slice is observable everywhere.
        readonly = [ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{slice_triple}" / "program" / "soffice.bin"
                    for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES]
        for member in readonly:
            member.chmod(stat.S_IRUSR | stat.S_IRGRP | stat.S_IROTH)
        staged_member = uroot / RESOURCES_REL / "libreoffice" / "soffice.bin"
        try:
            merged_modes = _modes_at_rename(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:],
                                  runner=_LipoRunner())
            )
        finally:
            # Restored the way `_modes_at_rename` restores `os.replace`: a fixture that mutates
            # the environment puts it back on the failing path too, or the next leg inherits it.
            for member in [*readonly, staged_member]:
                if member.exists():
                    member.chmod(stat.S_IRUSR | stat.S_IWUSR)
        _record(
            "universal staging: the merged MODE is in place AT the rename, not applied after it",
            # The ordering `finalize` exists for. Applied after `os.replace` the FINAL file is
            # identical, which is why the "keeps its mode" legs cannot see the difference - and
            # why an interrupted run leaving a merged file under the universal name with the
            # wrong mode was invisible on every platform, not just this one.
            len(merged_modes) == 1 and not (merged_modes[0] & stat.S_IWUSR),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
            (ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{slice_triple}" / "ffmpeg").write_bytes(_FAT_BOTH)
        copied_modes = _modes_at_rename(
            lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[:1],
                              runner=_LipoRunner())
        )
        _record(
            "universal staging: the COPIED sidecar's mode is in place at the rename too",
            # Exec-bit here, so POSIX-only: the copy path's own publish sets the bit rather than
            # inheriting a mode, and there is no read-only source to observe instead.
            # The already-universal branch has its own copy-and-publish, so it has its own
            # ordering to get wrong; the fixture writes the slices without an exec bit, so the
            # observed one can only come from the staging step.
            len(copied_modes) == 1
            and (bool(copied_modes[0] & stat.S_IXUSR) if os.name != "nt" else True),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
            shutil.rmtree(ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{slice_triple}" / "program")
            (ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{slice_triple}" / "program").mkdir()
        stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS[1:], runner=_LipoRunner())
        _record(
            "universal staging: two EMPTY slice trees still produce the destination directory",
            # `copytree` on the per-arch path creates it unconditionally, and `relatives()`'s
            # contract is that the universal tree does not differ from the three per-arch ones.
            # A merge loop over an empty path set creates nothing, so the corner needs saying.
            (uroot / RESOURCES_REL / "libreoffice").is_dir(),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        base = Path(tmp)
        (base / "a").write_bytes(b"AAAA" + b"BBBB")
        (base / "b").write_bytes(b"AAAA" + b"CCCC")
        (base / "c").write_bytes(b"AAAA" + b"BBBB")
        (base / "short").write_bytes(b"AAAA")
        _saved_chunk = _COMPARE_CHUNK
        globals()["_COMPARE_CHUNK"] = 4
        try:
            differs_late = same_bytes(base / "a", base / "b")
            agrees = same_bytes(base / "a", base / "c")
            prefix = same_bytes(base / "a", base / "short")
            prefix_reversed = same_bytes(base / "short", base / "a")
        finally:
            globals()["_COMPARE_CHUNK"] = _saved_chunk
        _record(
            "same_bytes: the comparison spans CHUNKS, and a prefix is not a match",
            # Driven across a chunk boundary, which no engine fixture can do at a 1 MiB read: a
            # comparison that stopped after the first chunk would be correct on every one of them
            # and would silently take the already-universal COPY path on two differing slices.
            # The third case is the size difference the function no longer short-circuits.
            differs_late is False
            and agrees is True
            # BOTH directions. Asserted long-side-first only, moving the EOF check above the
            # inequality check survives - and then a short side whose length is an exact multiple
            # of the chunk answers True against a longer file, which at the tree call site turns
            # the "differs but has no merge rule" refusal into a silent publish of the shorter
            # slice's bytes.
            and prefix is False
            and prefix_reversed is False
            # ...and the seam this leg rests on is REAL: hard-coding the read size back into the
            # body would leave every assertion above true while the loop was never crossed, so
            # the leg would pass vacuously. Structural, because the chunk size cannot change an
            # answer - that is precisely why it needs pinning some other way.
            and "_COMPARE_CHUNK" in same_bytes.__code__.co_names,
        )

    _absent_lipo = None
    try:
        run_tool(["convertia-no-such-tool-in-any-PATH", "-create"])
    except StagingError as e:
        _absent_lipo = e
    _record(
        "an absent `lipo` is a structural refusal (exit 2), not a filesystem fault (exit 1)",
        # `subprocess` raises FileNotFoundError, an OSError, which `main` reports as exit 1
        # alongside genuine filesystem faults - the module docstring reserves exit 2 for an
        # unusable prerequisite, and "the tool is not installed" is precisely that. The two read
        # very differently to whoever is looking at a red macOS leg.
        _absent_lipo is not None
        and "is not on PATH" in str(_absent_lipo)
        # ...naming the program it actually failed to spawn. Hard-coding `lipo` back reads
        # identically on the one call site that exists and lies the moment a second one does.
        and "convertia-no-such-tool-in-any-PATH" in str(_absent_lipo)
        and _absent_lipo.structural,
    )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        # P3: `row.member` picks the file INSIDE the entry, and every universal fixture row left
        # it None - so the universal sidecar path's own resolution was never driven.
        member_rows = (
            EngineStaging("ffmpeg", StagingKind.SIDECAR, "ffprobe", member="bin/ffprobe"),
        )
        for slice_triple in UNIVERSAL_SLICES:
            entry = ucache / f"ffmpeg-7.1-{slice_triple}" / "bin"
            entry.mkdir()
            (entry / "ffprobe").write_bytes(_THIN_MACHO + slice_triple.encode())
        member_runner = _LipoRunner()
        stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, member_rows, runner=member_runner)
        _record(
            "universal staging: a sidecar row's `member` picks the file inside the entry",
            # Resolved a second time on this path, so it can disagree with the per-arch one.
            len(member_runner.calls) == 1
            and all(call.endswith("bin/ffprobe") or call.endswith("bin\\ffprobe")
                    for call in member_runner.calls[0][2:4])
            and (uroot / BINARIES_REL / f"ffprobe-{UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE}").is_file(),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        absent_rows = (EngineStaging("pandoc", StagingKind.SIDECAR, "pandoc"),)
        absent = stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, absent_rows, runner=_LipoRunner())
        _record(
            "universal staging: an engine absent from BOTH slices is reported, not an error",
            # §6.1.3 lets a platform ship without a §3.4-unavailable engine, so absent-everywhere is
            # a REPORT while absent-from-one is the P4.29.1 refusal. Driven through stage_all,
            # because the leg on `_slice_entries` alone never sees what the caller does with None.
            absent == [StagedResult(absent_rows[0], None, None)],
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        check_runner = _LipoRunner()
        stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS, write=False,
                  runner=check_runner)
        _record(
            "universal --check resolves and validates but merges nothing",
            check_runner.calls == []
            and not (uroot / BINARIES_REL / f"ffmpeg-{UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE}").exists(),
        )
        (ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{UNIVERSAL_SLICES[0]}" / "program" / "extra.rdb").write_bytes(b"x")
        _record(
            "universal staging: a path present in only ONE slice's tree is refused",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS,
                                  runner=_LipoRunner()),
                "do not carry the same paths",
            ),
        )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        uroot, ucache = _universal_fixture(Path(tmp))
        for slice_triple, payload in zip(UNIVERSAL_SLICES, (b"one", b"two")):
            (ucache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{slice_triple}" / "program" / "shared.rdb").write_bytes(payload)
        _record(
            "universal staging: a DIFFERING non-Mach-O has no merge rule and is refused",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(uroot, ucache, UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE, _UNIVERSAL_ROWS,
                                  runner=_LipoRunner()),
                "no merge rule for it",
            ),
        )

    _record("target: unknown triple refused", _raises(lambda: resolve_target("mips-unknown-linux-gnu"), "unknown target triple"))
    # A host outside _HOST_TRIPLES (Linux aarch64, Windows ARM64) must REPORT, not traceback.
    host = host_target_triple()
    _record(
        "target: host resolution agrees with the table",
        resolve_target(None) == host if host is not None else _raises(lambda: resolve_target(None), "no §3.4.5 v1 target triple for this host"),
    )

    # (3) cache-key parsing: dashes in both the engine name and the version must still parse.
    _record(
        "cache key: dashed version parses",
        cache_entry_matches("ffmpeg-7.1-full_build-" + _LINUX, "ffmpeg", _LINUX),
    )
    _record(
        "cache key: dashed engine name parses",
        cache_entry_matches("convertia-imgworker-0.1.0-" + _LINUX, "convertia-imgworker", _LINUX),
    )
    _record(
        "cache key: empty version rejected",
        not cache_entry_matches("ffmpeg--" + _LINUX, "ffmpeg", _LINUX),
    )
    _record(
        "cache key: other triple rejected",
        not cache_entry_matches("ffmpeg-7.1-" + _WIN, "ffmpeg", _LINUX),
    )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        base = Path(tmp)
        root, cache = _fixture_tree(base)

        # (4) the positive path: placement, triple-suffixing, resources tree, exec bit.
        results = stage_all(root, cache, _LINUX, _FIXTURE_ROWS)
        ffmpeg = root / BINARIES_REL / f"ffmpeg-{_LINUX}"
        ffprobe = root / BINARIES_REL / f"ffprobe-{_LINUX}"
        tree = root / RESOURCES_REL / "libreoffice" / "program" / "soffice.bin"
        _record("stage: every declared row resolved", len(results) == 3 and all(r.dest for r in results))
        _record("stage: sidecars land triple-suffixed in src-tauri/binaries", ffmpeg.is_file() and ffprobe.is_file())
        _record("stage: two sidecars from ONE cache entry", ffmpeg.read_bytes() != ffprobe.read_bytes())
        _record("stage: resource tree lands under src-tauri/resources/<subdir>", tree.is_file())
        _record(
            "stage: exec bit set on a non-Windows sidecar",
            bool(ffmpeg.stat().st_mode & stat.S_IXUSR) if os.name != "nt" else True,
        )

        # (5) idempotence: a re-run over an already-staged tree is clean, not a duplicate/failure.
        stage_all(root, cache, _LINUX, _FIXTURE_ROWS)
        _record("stage: re-run is idempotent", ffmpeg.is_file() and tree.is_file())

        # (5b) a re-stage REPLACES the resource tree: a file the bumped version dropped must not
        # linger and ship (P4.41's manifest + G35's SBOM are generated FROM what is staged).
        stale = root / RESOURCES_REL / "libreoffice" / "program" / "dropped-by-the-bump"
        stale.write_bytes(b"stale")
        stage_all(root, cache, _LINUX, _FIXTURE_ROWS)
        _record(
            "stage: a re-stage replaces the tree, leaving no stale file",
            not stale.exists() and tree.is_file(),
        )

        # (5b2) SEVERAL rows sharing one destination must ACCUMULATE, not erase each other:
        # §3.3.1 maps the whole image stack onto `resources/image/`, so P5.1/P5.3/P5.5/P5.8/
        # P5.11/P5.13 each stage their own cache entry into that one subdir. A per-ROW clear
        # would leave only the last row's files - up to and including ImageMagick's `policy.xml`,
        # G37's only structural coder-lockdown. This leg is what pins the per-DESTINATION clear.
        for eng, lib in (("libvips", "libvips.so"), ("libheif", "libheif.so")):
            libdir = cache / f"{eng}-1.0-{_LINUX}" / "lib"
            libdir.mkdir(parents=True)
            (libdir / lib).write_bytes(b"image-stack component")
        shared_rows = (
            EngineStaging("libvips", StagingKind.RESOURCE_TREE, "image"),
            EngineStaging("libheif", StagingKind.RESOURCE_TREE, "image"),
        )
        stage_all(root, cache, _LINUX, shared_rows)
        image_lib = sorted(p.name for p in (root / RESOURCES_REL / "image" / "lib").iterdir())
        _record(
            "stage: rows sharing one destination accumulate, they do not erase each other",
            image_lib == ["libheif.so", "libvips.so"],
        )
        # ...and the per-destination clear still drops what a bump removed, across the group.
        gone = root / RESOURCES_REL / "image" / "lib" / "dropped-by-the-bump.so"
        gone.write_bytes(b"stale")
        stage_all(root, cache, _LINUX, shared_rows)
        _record(
            "stage: the per-destination clear still removes a stale file from a shared subdir",
            not gone.exists()
            and sorted(p.name for p in (root / RESOURCES_REL / "image" / "lib").iterdir())
            == ["libheif.so", "libvips.so"],
        )

        # (5c) symlinks inside a cache entry are PRESERVED (never dereferenced - that would copy
        # an out-of-entry target INTO the bundle), and a link RESOLVING outside the staged tree is
        # REFUSED (preserving is not a refusal; the packers and P4.41's manifest read follow it).
        # §0.11 T3a. Skipped as a recorded PASS where the OS refuses to create a symlink
        # (unprivileged Windows), so the leg count stays stable for the g24 canary (landed
        # 2026-08-31), which asserts the skip REASON per OS, not merely the tally, since these
        # are the suite's only unconditionally-true legs.
        outside = base / "outside-the-entry.txt"
        outside.write_bytes(b"out-of-entry sentinel")
        program = cache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{_LINUX}" / "program"
        inner, escape = program / "inner.link", program / "escape.link"
        try:
            # RELATIVE, like every internal link in a real LibreOffice/.app tree - an ABSOLUTE
            # link would still point into the build machine's cache entry after the copy, i.e.
            # outside the bundle, which is precisely what the escape leg below asserts is refused.
            inner.symlink_to("soffice.bin")
        except (OSError, NotImplementedError):
            note("this host cannot create a symlink; the three symlink legs have no subject")
            _record("stage: symlink preserve leg (skipped - host cannot create symlinks)", True)
            _record("fail-closed: symlink escape leg (skipped - host cannot create symlinks)", True)
            _record("fail-closed: absolute-symlink leg (skipped - host cannot create symlinks)", True)
        else:
            stage_all(root, cache, _LINUX, _FIXTURE_ROWS)
            staged_link = root / RESOURCES_REL / "libreoffice" / "program" / "inner.link"
            _record(
                "stage: an internal symlink is preserved, not dereferenced",
                staged_link.is_symlink(),
            )
            escape.symlink_to(outside)
            _record(
                "fail-closed: a staged symlink resolving outside the staged tree",
                _raises(
                    lambda: stage_all(root, cache, _LINUX, _FIXTURE_ROWS),
                    "is a link resolving outside the staged tree",
                ),
            )
            escape.unlink()
            # An ABSOLUTE link pointing INSIDE its own entry passes containment, yet `copytree`
            # preserves it verbatim so the staged bundle would carry the build machine's
            # `.engine-cache/` path. This is the class a source-side-only containment check drops.
            absolute = program / "absolute.link"
            absolute.symlink_to(program / "soffice.bin")
            _record(
                "fail-closed: an absolute symlink, even one pointing inside its own entry",
                _raises(
                    lambda: stage_all(root, cache, _LINUX, _FIXTURE_ROWS),
                    "is an ABSOLUTE symlink",
                ),
            )
            absolute.unlink()
            inner.unlink()

        # (5d) a Windows DIRECTORY JUNCTION is NOT a symlink: `copytree` keys on is_symlink(), so
        # it recurses INTO a junction and materialises the out-of-entry content as a plain
        # directory - by the time the staged tree exists there is no link left to detect. That is
        # exactly why the guard runs on the SOURCE. Real junction, real refusal.
        if os.name == "nt":
            outside_dir = base / "outside-dir"
            outside_dir.mkdir()
            (outside_dir / "secret.txt").write_bytes(b"out-of-entry sentinel")
            junction = program / "junction.link"
            made = subprocess.run(
                ["cmd", "/c", "mklink", "/J", str(junction), str(outside_dir)],
                capture_output=True,
                check=False,
            )
            if made.returncode == 0:
                _record(
                    "fail-closed: a Windows directory junction resolving outside the tree",
                    _raises(
                        lambda: stage_all(root, cache, _LINUX, _FIXTURE_ROWS, write=False),
                        "is a link resolving outside the staged tree",
                    ),
                )
                junction.rmdir()  # removes the junction, never the target
                # The ALLOW direction, which the refuse leg alone cannot see: `os.readlink`
                # succeeds on a junction but hands back a `\\?\`-prefixed path that
                # `Path.resolve()` leaves un-normalised, so containment could never hold and an
                # INTERNAL junction was refused with a wrong diagnosis - a Windows-only staging
                # hard-fail at P5-P7. `_link_target` uses `os.path.realpath` for exactly this.
                inside = program / "real"
                inside.mkdir()
                (inside / "inside.txt").write_bytes(b"a genuinely internal target")
                internal = program / "internal.link"
                made2 = subprocess.run(
                    ["cmd", "/c", "mklink", "/J", str(internal), str(inside)],
                    capture_output=True,
                    check=False,
                )
                if made2.returncode == 0:
                    allowed = True
                    try:
                        stage_all(root, cache, _LINUX, _FIXTURE_ROWS, write=False)
                    except StagingError:
                        allowed = False
                    _record("an internal Windows junction is allowed, not falsely refused", allowed)
                    internal.rmdir()
                else:
                    _record("junction allow leg (skipped - mklink unavailable)", True)
                shutil.rmtree(inside)
                # ...and a junction at its own ANCESTOR passes containment yet would make
                # `copytree`'s materialisation recurse until the filesystem refuses.
                cycle = program / "cycle.link"
                made3 = subprocess.run(
                    ["cmd", "/c", "mklink", "/J", str(cycle), str(cache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{_LINUX}")],
                    capture_output=True,
                    check=False,
                )
                if made3.returncode == 0:
                    _record(
                        "fail-closed: a junction pointing at its own ancestor (the copy would loop)",
                        _raises(
                            lambda: stage_all(root, cache, _LINUX, _FIXTURE_ROWS, write=False),
                            "is a junction pointing at its own ancestor",
                        ),
                    )
                    cycle.rmdir()
                else:
                    _record("junction cycle leg (skipped - mklink unavailable)", True)
            else:
                note("this host refused `mklink /J`; the three junction legs have no subject")
                _record("fail-closed: junction escape leg (skipped - mklink unavailable)", True)
                _record("junction allow leg (skipped - mklink unavailable)", True)
                _record("junction cycle leg (skipped - mklink unavailable)", True)
        else:
            _record("fail-closed: junction escape leg (skipped - Windows-only)", True)
            _record("junction allow leg (skipped - junctions are Windows-only)", True)
            _record("junction cycle leg (skipped - junctions are Windows-only)", True)

        stage_all(root, cache, _LINUX, _FIXTURE_ROWS)

        # (6) --check / --dry-run resolves + validates but writes nothing.
        dry_root = base / "dry"
        (dry_root / BINARIES_REL).mkdir(parents=True)
        (dry_root / RESOURCES_REL).mkdir(parents=True)
        stage_all(dry_root, cache, _LINUX, _FIXTURE_ROWS, write=False)
        _record(
            "check: nothing written on write=False",
            not any((dry_root / BINARIES_REL).iterdir()) and not any((dry_root / RESOURCES_REL).iterdir()),
        )

        # (7) an engine absent from the cache is REPORTED, never invented.
        absent_rows = _FIXTURE_ROWS + (EngineStaging("pandoc", StagingKind.SIDECAR, "pandoc"),)
        absent = stage_all(root, cache, _LINUX, absent_rows, write=False)
        _record(
            "absent engine reported, not staged",
            sum(1 for r in absent if r.dest is None) == 1
            and not (root / BINARIES_REL / f"pandoc-{_LINUX}").exists(),
        )

        # (8) the fail-closed legs.
        _record(
            "fail-closed: a resource subdir outside the §3.3.1 key set",
            _raises(
                lambda: resource_dest(root, "ghostscript"),
                "is not a §3.3.1 `bundle.resources` source key",
            ),
        )
        # An entry no row claims is a build/link input (the P4.34 ruling), NOT a staging error.
        stage_all(root, cache, _LINUX, _FIXTURE_ROWS[:2], write=False)
        unclaimed = unclaimed_cache_entries(cache, _FIXTURE_ROWS[:2], _LINUX)
        _record(
            "unclaimed cache entry reported, not failed (the P4.34 link-input class)",
            f"libreoffice-24.8-{_LINUX}" in unclaimed
            and f"ffmpeg-7.1-full_build-{_LINUX}" not in unclaimed,
        )
        _record(
            "fail-closed: one entry claimed by two declared engines (the dash-prefix collision)",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(
                    root, cache, _LINUX,
                    _FIXTURE_ROWS + (EngineStaging("ffmpeg-7.1", StagingKind.SIDECAR, "x"),),
                    write=False,
                ),
                "parses as belonging to more than one declared engine",
            ),
        )
        (cache / f"ffmpeg-7.0-{_LINUX}").mkdir()
        _record(
            "fail-closed: two pinned versions of one engine restored",
            _raises(
                lambda: select_cache_entry(cache, "ffmpeg", _LINUX),
                "ambiguous engine-asset cache",
            ),
        )
        shutil.rmtree(cache / f"ffmpeg-7.0-{_LINUX}")
        _record(
            "fail-closed: a declared member missing from its cache entry",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(
                    root, cache, _LINUX,
                    _FIXTURE_ROWS + (EngineStaging("libreoffice", StagingKind.SIDECAR, "soffice"),),
                    write=False,
                ),
                # `stage_sidecar`'s own prefix: "is missing from the restored cache entry"
                # alone is shared with the resource-tree arm, which the same call can reach.
                "sidecar 'soffice'",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "fail-closed: a declared resource SUBTREE missing from its cache entry",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(
                    root,
                    cache,
                    _LINUX,
                    (EngineStaging("libreoffice", StagingKind.RESOURCE_TREE, "libreoffice", "absent"),),
                    write=False,
                ),
                "a `bundle.resources` row stages a DIRECTORY",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "fail-closed: a member path escaping its cache entry",
            _raises(
                lambda: _member_path(
                    cache / f"libreoffice-24.8-{_LINUX}", "../ffmpeg-7.1-full_build-" + _LINUX
                ),
                "escapes its entry",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "fail-closed: an absent cache root",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(
                    root, base / "no-such-cache", _LINUX, _FIXTURE_ROWS, write=False
                ),
                "does not exist",
            ),
        )

        # --- the STAGED_ENGINES <-> engines.lock binder (the (A′) ruling) --------------------
        lock = base / "engines.lock"
        lock.write_bytes(b"# no rows yet\n")
        _record(
            "binder: an EMPTY manifest binds nothing - fail-open until the first row lands",
            manifest_cache_engines(lock) is None
            and unbound_staging_rows(_FIXTURE_ROWS, lock) == [],
        )
        _record(
            "binder: an ABSENT manifest is fail-open too",
            manifest_cache_engines(base / "no-such.lock") is None,
        )
        # The declared token must DIFFER from the id, or the "declared wins" half proves nothing:
        # with `id == cache_engine` the leg stays green even if `cache_engine` is ignored entirely.
        lock.write_text(
            '[[engine]]\nid = "ffprobe"\ncache_engine = "ffmpeg"\n\n'
            '[[engine]]\nid = "libreoffice"\n',
            encoding="utf-8",
        )
        _record(
            "binder: a declared cache_engine wins over the id",
            manifest_cache_engines(lock) == {"ffmpeg", "libreoffice"}
            and "ffprobe" not in manifest_cache_engines(lock),
        )
        _record(
            "binder: an UNdeclared cache_engine falls back to the id",
            manifest_cache_engines(lock) is not None
            and "libreoffice" in manifest_cache_engines(lock),
        )
        _record(
            "binder: malformed manifest TOML is an error, not a silent fail-open",
            _raises(
                lambda: manifest_cache_engines(_write(base / "broken.lock", "[[engine]\n")),
                "not valid TOML",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "binder: a staging row naming an entry no manifest row declares is reported",
            unbound_staging_rows(
                (*_FIXTURE_ROWS, EngineStaging("libheif", StagingKind.SIDECAR, "libheif")), lock
            )
            == ["libheif"],
        )
        _record(
            "binder: staging rows whose entries ARE declared are not reported",
            unbound_staging_rows(_FIXTURE_ROWS, lock) == [],
        )
        _record(
            "binder: the real repo manifest EXISTS and currently binds nothing",
            (ROOT / "src-tauri" / "engines.lock").is_file()
            and manifest_cache_engines(ROOT / "src-tauri" / "engines.lock") is None,
        )
        # A FRESH cache root, and the arm named: the shared `cache` fixture already carries a
        # half-populated libheif entry from an earlier leg, so `stage_all` would raise from the
        # member-resolution arm whether or not the binder ran - which is how this leg first shipped
        # green with the binder disconnected.
        binder_cache = base / "cache-binder"
        binder_cache.mkdir()
        _record(
            "binder: stage_all REFUSES an unbound row (the check runs on the real path)",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(
                    root,
                    binder_cache,
                    _LINUX,
                    (EngineStaging("libheif", StagingKind.SIDECAR, "libheif"),),
                    write=False,
                    lock_path=lock,
                ),
                "name a §6.1.3 cache entry no `engines.lock`",
            ),
        )
        # Fail-OPEN with a NON-empty row set: `rows=()` would make this true however the empty-
        # manifest branch behaved, which is no assertion at all.
        # The PACKAGE-TIME path: `main()` is where `lock_path` is actually wired, and a
        # ROOT-correct mutation showed that dropping it survived the whole suite - the binder was
        # pinned inside `stage_all` but nothing pinned that `main()` passes it. Driving `main()`
        # with a patched STAGED_ENGINES covers the wiring AND `report()` in one go.
        main_base = base / "main-path"
        main_root, main_cache = _fixture_tree(main_base)
        (main_root / "src-tauri").mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        main_lock = main_root / LOCK_REL
        # The P4.31 bundle binder runs on this same path, so the fixture tree needs the config the
        # real repo has. Declared BOUND here, so the legs below that move the manifest still test
        # the manifest. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.31]
        _main_res = {f"{BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX}libreoffice/": "engines/libreoffice/"}
        _main_bin = [f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}ffmpeg", f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}ffprobe"]
        _conf(main_root / TAURI_CONF_REL, _main_bin, _main_res)
        argv = [
            "--root", str(main_root),
            "--cache-root", str(main_cache),
            "--target", _LINUX,
            "--check",
            "-v",
        ]
        declared = globals()["STAGED_ENGINES"]
        globals()["STAGED_ENGINES"] = _FIXTURE_ROWS
        try:
            main_lock.write_text(
                '[[engine]]\nid = "ffmpeg"\n\n[[engine]]\nid = "libreoffice"\n', encoding="utf-8"
            )
            bound_rc = main([*argv])
            main_lock.write_text('[[engine]]\nid = "ffmpeg"\n', encoding="utf-8")
            unbound_rc = main([*argv])
            main_lock.write_bytes(b"# no rows yet\n")
            open_rc = main([*argv])
            # The CONFIG side of the same wiring. The manifest is left fail-OPEN by the line
            # above, so nothing but the bundle binder can decide this run - which is what makes it
            # a leg for `main()` passing `conf_path` rather than for the manifest binder again.
            # [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.31]
            _conf(main_root / TAURI_CONF_REL, [f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}ffmpeg"], {})
            conf_unbound_rc = main([*argv])
        finally:
            globals()["STAGED_ENGINES"] = declared
        # The NO-ROWS early return is its OWN wiring: `main` returns before `stage_all`, so the
        # binder inside `stage_all` can never speak there - yet a config entry naming bytes nothing
        # stages is refused already, which is what makes the reverse direction live before the
        # first P5 row exists. Driven with an EXPLICITLY empty row set rather than by relying on
        # the real one still being empty: the first P5-P7 row would otherwise send these two legs
        # down the staging path and red them under names that no longer describe what they
        # exercise. (That the REAL set is empty today is the separate live leg above, which reads
        # the real config against `STAGED_ENGINES` directly.) [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.31]
        globals()["STAGED_ENGINES"] = ()
        try:
            _conf(main_root / TAURI_CONF_REL, [f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}ffmpeg"], {})
            norows_unbound_rc = main([*argv])
            _conf(main_root / TAURI_CONF_REL, [], {})
            norows_open_rc = main([*argv])
        finally:
            globals()["STAGED_ENGINES"] = declared
        _record("main(): a fully bound row set stages cleanly", bound_rc == 0)
        _record(
            "main(): an UNbound row set is refused at package time (the wiring is live)",
            unbound_rc == 1,
        )
        _record("main(): an empty manifest is fail-OPEN at package time too", open_rc == 0)
        _record(
            "main(): a row set unbound to `tauri.conf.json` is refused at package time too - the "
            "bundle binder is wired, not just written",
            conf_unbound_rc == 1,
        )
        _record(
            "main(): the NO-ROWS early return checks the config as well - an `externalBin` entry "
            "nothing stages is refused while STAGED_ENGINES is still empty",
            norows_unbound_rc == 1,
        )
        _record(
            "main(): the NO-ROWS early return stays fail-OPEN when the config declares nothing "
            "either - the leg above cannot be passing for the wrong reason",
            norows_open_rc == 0,
        )

        _record(
            "binder: a row that names NO cache entry fails CLOSED, not fail-open",
            _raises(
                lambda: manifest_cache_engines(
                    _write(base / "blank-id.lock", '[[engine]]\nid = "   "\n')
                ),
                "names no cache entry",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "binder: a DECLARED-but-unusable cache_engine is refused, not read as the id",
            _raises(
                lambda: manifest_cache_engines(
                    _write(base / "typed-token.lock", '[[engine]]\nid = "ffmpeg"\ncache_engine = []\n')
                ),
                "declared but unusable",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "binder: a non-table engine entry fails CLOSED too",
            _raises(
                lambda: manifest_cache_engines(_write(base / "scalar.lock", "engine = [1]\n")),
                "is not a table",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "binder: a mis-typed `engine` key fails CLOSED, unlike an absent one",
            _raises(
                lambda: manifest_cache_engines(
                    _write(base / "typed.lock", 'engine = "not an array"\n')
                ),
                "must be an array of tables",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "binder: stage_all is fail-OPEN while the manifest declares no rows",
            stage_all(
                root,
                binder_cache,
                _LINUX,
                _FIXTURE_ROWS,
                write=False,
                lock_path=_write(base / "empty2.lock", "# none\n"),
            )
            == [StagedResult(row, None, None) for row in _FIXTURE_ROWS],
        )

        # --- the STAGED_ENGINES <-> tauri.conf.json bundle binder (P4.31) --------------------
        # The forward direction is the SILENT one: bytes staged into src-tauri/binaries/ that no
        # `externalBin` entry names are simply not packaged, `tauri build` succeeds, and the miss
        # surfaces as a runtime EngineMissing in a shipped artifact. The reverse direction does
        # reach `tauri-build`'s own copy step, but one compile later and phrased as a missing file
        # rather than as two declarations having drifted. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.31]
        _BOUND_BIN = [f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}ffmpeg", f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}ffprobe"]
        _BOUND_RES = {f"{BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX}libreoffice/": "engines/libreoffice/"}
        conf_dir = base / "conf"
        _record(
            "bundle binder: the two §3.3.1 keys are READ back as declared",
            bundle_entries(_conf(conf_dir / "ok.json", _BOUND_BIN, _BOUND_RES))
            == (tuple(_BOUND_BIN), _BOUND_RES),
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: a config with NEITHER key present reads as two empty declarations, "
            "not as an error - the keys are optional in Tauri's own schema",
            bundle_entries(_write(conf_dir / "bare.json", '{"bundle": {}}')) == ((), {}),
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: an ABSENT config fails CLOSED, unlike the engines.lock reader - a "
            "committed required file that is missing means a broken checkout, not an empty phase",
            _raises(lambda: bundle_entries(conf_dir / "no-such.json"), "cannot read the Tauri config"),
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: malformed JSON fails CLOSED",
            _raises(
                lambda: bundle_entries(_write(conf_dir / "broken.json", "{not json")),
                "is not valid JSON",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: a non-object `bundle` fails CLOSED",
            _raises(
                lambda: bundle_entries(_write(conf_dir / "scalar.json", '{"bundle": 7}')),
                "`bundle` must be an object",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: a non-string-array `externalBin` fails CLOSED",
            _raises(
                lambda: bundle_entries(
                    _write(conf_dir / "badbin.json", '{"bundle": {"externalBin": [1]}}')
                ),
                "must be an array of strings",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: a non-string-map `resources` fails CLOSED",
            _raises(
                lambda: bundle_entries(
                    _write(conf_dir / "badres.json", '{"bundle": {"resources": {"a": 1}}}')
                ),
                "must be a source->target object of strings",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: a fully bound pair reports NOTHING (no over-fire)",
            unbound_bundle_entries(_FIXTURE_ROWS, tuple(_BOUND_BIN), _BOUND_RES) == [],
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder (forward, sidecar): a SIDECAR row no `externalBin` entry names is "
            "reported - the silent half, since Tauri simply does not package what it is not told "
            "about",
            unbound_bundle_entries(
                _FIXTURE_ROWS, (f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}ffmpeg",), _BOUND_RES
            )
            == ["sidecar row 'ffprobe' has no `bundle.externalBin` entry (expected "
                f"'{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}ffprobe')"],
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder (forward, tree): a RESOURCE_TREE row no `resources` key names is "
            "reported",
            unbound_bundle_entries(_FIXTURE_ROWS, tuple(_BOUND_BIN), {})
            == ["resource row 'libreoffice' has no `bundle.resources` key (expected "
                f"'{BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX}libreoffice/')"],
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder (reverse, sidecar): an `externalBin` entry no row stages is reported",
            unbound_bundle_entries(
                _FIXTURE_ROWS, (*_BOUND_BIN, f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}pandoc"), _BOUND_RES
            )
            == [f"`bundle.externalBin` entry '{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}pandoc' names no staging row"],
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder (reverse, tree): a `resources/<subdir>/` key no row stages is reported",
            unbound_bundle_entries(
                _FIXTURE_ROWS,
                tuple(_BOUND_BIN),
                {**_BOUND_RES, f"{BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX}image/": "engines/image/"},
            )
            == [f"`bundle.resources` key '{BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX}image/' names no staging row"],
        )
        # The two SHAPE legs. A binder that only compared the member sets either side declares
        # would pass both of these: the first because the bare name matches a row, the second
        # because a mistyped subdir is not a member of anything and would fall out of the
        # comparison entirely. Prefix-as-a-rule is what makes them reachable at all.
        _record(
            "bundle binder (shape): an `externalBin` entry OUTSIDE `binaries/` is refused even "
            "though its bare name matches a staging row - Tauri would resolve it against a path "
            "this script never writes",
            unbound_bundle_entries(_FIXTURE_ROWS, ("ffmpeg", *_BOUND_BIN), _BOUND_RES)
            == [f"`bundle.externalBin` entry 'ffmpeg' is not under `{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}` - "
                "§3.3.2 step 2 stages every sidecar there, so Tauri would resolve it against a "
                "path nothing writes"],
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder (shape): an un-prefixed entry does not COUNT as the row's declaration "
            "either - the row is ALSO reported undeclared, so the diagnostic says what to write "
            "and not only what is wrong. Measured: without this leg, dropping the prefix filter "
            "from the declared set survived the whole suite, because the leg above hands it a "
            "correctly-prefixed entry as well",
            unbound_bundle_entries((_FIXTURE_ROWS[0],), ("ffmpeg",), {})
            == ["sidecar row 'ffmpeg' has no `bundle.externalBin` entry (expected "
                f"'{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}ffmpeg')",
                f"`bundle.externalBin` entry 'ffmpeg' is not under `{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}` - "
                "§3.3.2 step 2 stages every sidecar there, so Tauri would resolve it against a "
                "path nothing writes"],
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder (shape): a MISTYPED resource subdir is reported, not skipped as an "
            "unrecognised key",
            unbound_bundle_entries(
                _FIXTURE_ROWS,
                tuple(_BOUND_BIN),
                {f"{BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX}libreofice/": "engines/libreoffice/"},
            )
            == ["resource row 'libreoffice' has no `bundle.resources` key (expected "
                f"'{BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX}libreoffice/')",
                f"`bundle.resources` key '{BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX}libreofice/' names no staging row"],
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: NON-engine map entries are not claimed - the same map carries the "
            "§3.3.1 licences text and the §3.3.2 step 4 SBOM/NOTICE + patent-disposition record, "
            "which no staging row produces",
            unbound_bundle_entries(
                _FIXTURE_ROWS,
                tuple(_BOUND_BIN),
                {**_BOUND_RES, "../THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt": "", "../NOTICE": ""},
            )
            == [],
        )
        # The sidecar side's own boundary. §3.3.1 lists six externalBin entries and one of them,
        # `convertia-imgworker`, is a first-party cargo product (P4.34) that can never carry a
        # staging row - a row binds to an `engines.lock` entry, and that manifest requires an
        # `upstream_url` + `source_ref` a first-party build has none of. Without the exemption this
        # guard would meet P4.34 with no exit but a fake row or a weakened check; without the
        # collision leg the exemption could quietly shadow a real drift.
        _record(
            "bundle binder: a BUILT_SIDECARS entry needs no staging row - the reverse direction's "
            "declared escape hatch, so §3.3.1's own `binaries/convertia-imgworker` does not red a "
            "guard that cannot be satisfied",
            unbound_bundle_entries(
                _FIXTURE_ROWS,
                (*_BOUND_BIN, f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}{BUILT_SIDECARS[0]}"),
                _BOUND_RES,
            )
            == [],
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: the hatch is NAMED, not a pattern - a sidecar entry outside "
            "BUILT_SIDECARS is still refused, so a typo cannot hide behind the exemption",
            unbound_bundle_entries(
                _FIXTURE_ROWS, (*_BOUND_BIN, f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}imgworker"), _BOUND_RES
            )
            == [f"`bundle.externalBin` entry '{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}imgworker' names no staging row"],
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: a name that is BOTH exempt and staged is itself reported - the "
            "exemption exists for sidecars this script does not produce, so the collision means "
            "one of the two declarations is wrong",
            unbound_bundle_entries(
                (EngineStaging("x", StagingKind.SIDECAR, BUILT_SIDECARS[0]),),
                (f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}{BUILT_SIDECARS[0]}",),
                {},
            )
            == [f"'{BUILT_SIDECARS[0]}' is declared a first-party build product (BUILT_SIDECARS) "
                "AND staged by a row - one of the two is wrong, since the exemption is only for "
                "sidecars this script does not produce"],
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: the reverse resource check uses the FORWARD check's exact spelling - "
            "a doubled trailing slash claims no row, where an rstrip would have read it as a "
            "claim while the forward half still called the row undeclared",
            unbound_bundle_entries(
                _FIXTURE_ROWS, tuple(_BOUND_BIN), {f"{BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX}libreoffice//": "x/"}
            )
            == ["resource row 'libreoffice' has no `bundle.resources` key (expected "
                f"'{BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX}libreoffice/')",
                f"`bundle.resources` key '{BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX}libreoffice//' names no staging row"],
        )
        _separator_problems = unbound_bundle_entries(
            (), (), {"resources\\image\\": "engines/image/"}
        )
        _separator_arm = len(_separator_problems)
        _record(
            "bundle binder: a key that MEANS `resources/` without spelling it (a capital, a "
            "backslash) is reported as the mistyped destination it is, not skipped as a "
            "non-engine resource - it resolves on the authoring host and breaks on the runner",
            len(
                unbound_bundle_entries(
                    (), (), {"Resources/image/": "engines/image/"}
                )
            )
            == 1
            and "MEANS" in unbound_bundle_entries(
                (), (), {"Resources/image/": "engines/image/"}
            )[0]
            # BOTH enumerated arms carry the needle, not just the case one: `!= []` alone would
            # not say WHICH arm spoke, and a separator arm that fell through to some other message
            # would still satisfy it.
            and _separator_arm == 1
            and "MEANS" in _separator_problems[0]
            and unbound_bundle_entries((), (), {"../THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt": ""}) == [],
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: the BARE `resources` spelling means the whole staging tree and is "
            "reported too - Tauri would walk all of it into the resource root under the wrong "
            "internal paths - while a key that merely STARTS with the word stays non-engine",
            unbound_bundle_entries((), (), {"resources": ""}) != []
            and unbound_bundle_entries((), (), {"resources-readme.md": ""}) == [],
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: a JSON `null` reads as ABSENT, the way Tauri's own Option spells it - "
            "refusing it would reject a config Tauri accepts",
            bundle_entries(
                _write(conf_dir / "nulls.json",
                       '{"bundle": {"externalBin": null, "resources": null}}')
            )
            == ((), {}),
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: a DUPLICATE externalBin entry is reported once, not once per "
            "occurrence - JSON arrays permit duplicates where object keys do not",
            unbound_bundle_entries(
                _FIXTURE_ROWS,
                (*_BOUND_BIN, f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}pandoc", f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}pandoc"),
                _BOUND_RES,
            )
            == [f"`bundle.externalBin` entry '{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}pandoc' names no staging row"],
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: the two prefixes are DERIVED from the staging destinations, so a "
            "re-home of where this script writes cannot leave the config side behind",
            BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX == BINARIES_REL.split("/", 1)[1] + "/"
            and BUNDLE_RES_PREFIX == RESOURCES_REL.split("/", 1)[1] + "/"
            and BINARIES_REL.startswith(_CONF_DIR)
            and RESOURCES_REL.startswith(_CONF_DIR),
        )
        _both_conf = _conf(conf_dir / "both.json", [f"{BUNDLE_BIN_PREFIX}pandoc"], {})
        _record(
            "bundle binder: assert_rows_bound_to_bundle names EVERY problem it found, not the "
            "first - both directions arrive in one message",
            # ONE fixture, driven twice: `_raises` takes a single mandatory needle, so "the
            # message carries BOTH" is spelled as two calls over the same config rather than as
            # two configs that only look independent.
            _raises(
                lambda: assert_rows_bound_to_bundle(_FIXTURE_ROWS, _both_conf),
                "names no staging row",
            )
            and _raises(
                lambda: assert_rows_bound_to_bundle(_FIXTURE_ROWS, _both_conf),
                "has no `bundle.externalBin` entry",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: the REAL repo config parses and binds against the REAL row set - the "
            "live leg, which reds the moment a P5-P7 row is staged without its declaration",
            (ROOT / TAURI_CONF_REL).is_file()
            and unbound_bundle_entries(STAGED_ENGINES, *bundle_entries(ROOT / TAURI_CONF_REL))
            == [],
        )
        # The SEAM. Every guard above is driven directly; these two drive `stage_all`, which is
        # where the binder has to be reached to be a check at all. The manifest binder one section
        # up shipped GREEN while disconnected, and the P4.30 relocation tail did the same thing a
        # box later, so the wiring gets its own legs rather than being assumed from the guards'.
        bundle_cache = base / "cache-bundle"
        bundle_cache.mkdir()
        _record(
            "bundle binder: stage_all REFUSES an unbound row (the check runs on the real path)",
            _raises(
                lambda: stage_all(
                    root,
                    bundle_cache,
                    _LINUX,
                    _FIXTURE_ROWS,
                    write=False,
                    conf_path=_conf(conf_dir / "stage.json", [], {}),
                ),
                "has no `bundle.externalBin` entry",
            ),
        )
        _record(
            "bundle binder: stage_all is fail-OPEN when NEITHER side declares anything",
            stage_all(
                root,
                bundle_cache,
                _LINUX,
                (),
                write=False,
                conf_path=_conf(conf_dir / "empty.json", [], {}),
            )
            == [],
        )

    # (N) the §3.5 beside-the-exe load-path rewrite (P4.30). Every leg drives the injected runner
    # and an explicit triple, so all three per-OS legs are exercised on ANY host - required, since
    # the caged canary tolerates no skipped leg on POSIX, and a macOS-only mechanism checked only
    # on macOS would be checked on no CI leg this repo runs today.
    _MAC, _UNIV = "aarch64-apple-darwin", UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE
    _record(
        "relocation: every v1 triple AND the universal target declare a relocator",
        set(_RELOCATORS) == set(V1_TARGET_TRIPLES) | {UNIVERSAL_TRIPLE},
    )
    _record(
        "relocation: the read-back table covers exactly the same targets, so a new triple cannot "
        "arrive with a rewrite but no way to check it",
        set(_RELOCATION_READBACK) == set(_RELOCATORS),
    )
    _record(
        "relocation: an undeclared target is a structural refusal, never a silent no-op",
        _raises(lambda: relocator_for("riscv64-unknown-linux-gnu"),
                "no declared load-path relocation"),
    )
    _STAGE_LIB, _STAGE_DEP = Path("/stage/libmp3lame.0.dylib"), Path("/stage/ffmpeg")
    _lib = Relocation(dest="libmp3lame.so", set_id=True)
    _dep = Relocation(dest="ffmpeg", references=(("/opt/build/lib/libmp3lame.0.dylib",
                                                  "libmp3lame.0.dylib"),))
    _record(
        "relocation (macOS): a shipped lib gets `-id @loader_path/<its own final name>`",
        # The target path is asserted as PASS-THROUGH (`str(path)`), never re-spelled as a POSIX
        # literal: `str(Path("/stage/x"))` renders backslashes on Windows, and a leg written that
        # way fails on the dev host while claiming to be about install names.
        _macho_relocation_argv(_STAGE_LIB, _lib)
        == [["install_name_tool", "-id", "@loader_path/libmp3lame.0.dylib", str(_STAGE_LIB)]],
    )
    _record(
        "relocation (macOS): the dependent's ABSOLUTE reference is rewritten to @loader_path",
        _macho_relocation_argv(_STAGE_DEP, _dep)
        == [["install_name_tool", "-change", "/opt/build/lib/libmp3lame.0.dylib",
             "@loader_path/libmp3lame.0.dylib", str(_STAGE_DEP)]],
    )
    _record(
        "relocation (macOS): id and change travel in ONE invocation - the tool applies them "
        "together, so a half-rewritten Mach-O is not a reachable state",
        (lambda argv: len(argv) == 1 and argv[0].count("-change") == 2 and "-id" in argv[0])(
            _macho_relocation_argv(
                _STAGE_DEP,
                _dep._replace(set_id=True,
                              references=_dep.references + (("/opt/b/libogg.0.dylib",
                                                             "libogg.0.dylib"),)),
            )
        ),
    )
    _record(
        "relocation (macOS): `origin` shapes the prefix for a lib that does NOT sit beside the exe",
        _macho_relocation_argv(_STAGE_DEP, _lib._replace(origin="../lib"))[0][2]
        == "@loader_path/../lib/ffmpeg",
    )
    _record(
        "relocation (macOS): a declaration with no operation produces NO invocation",
        _macho_relocation_argv(_STAGE_DEP, Relocation(dest="ffmpeg")) == [],
    )
    _record(
        "relocation (Linux): `--set-rpath $ORIGIN` as ONE literal argv token, so the shell never "
        "sees the `$` (which is the only reason it survives to patchelf)",
        _elf_relocation_argv(_STAGE_DEP, _lib)
        == [["patchelf", "--set-rpath", "$ORIGIN", str(_STAGE_DEP)]],
    )
    _record(
        "relocation (Linux): `origin` shapes $ORIGIN/<sub>, and the leg needs no set_id/references "
        "because ELF has no per-lib install name to rewrite",
        _elf_relocation_argv(_STAGE_DEP, Relocation(dest="ffmpeg", origin="lib"))[0][2]
        == "$ORIGIN/lib",
    )
    _record(
        "relocation (Windows): a RECORDED no-op - declared in the table, so 'nothing to do' is "
        "distinguishable from 'nobody considered it'",
        _pe_relocation_argv(Path("/stage/ffmpeg.exe"), _lib) == []
        and _RELOCATORS[_WIN] is _pe_relocation_argv,
    )
    _record(
        "relocation: the read-back expectation is the FINAL name, never the staging spelling",
        expected_load_paths("libmp3lame.0.dylib", _lib, _MAC)
        == [("otool -D", "@loader_path/libmp3lame.0.dylib", "")]
        and expected_load_paths("ffmpeg", _lib, _LINUX)
        == [("patchelf --print-rpath", "$ORIGIN", "")],
    )
    _record(
        "relocation: EVERY declared operation is read back - the `-change` half through "
        "`otool -L`, because install_name_tool matches its old path literally and exits 0 on no "
        "match, which is the shape a rewrite most likely fails in",
        expected_load_paths("ffmpeg", _dep, _MAC)
        == [("otool -L", "@loader_path/libmp3lame.0.dylib",
             "/opt/build/lib/libmp3lame.0.dylib")],
    )
    _record(
        "relocation: a platform that rewrites NOTHING has nothing to read back either",
        expected_load_paths("ffmpeg.exe", _lib, _WIN) == [],
    )
    _record(
        "binder: a relocation naming no staged row is refused",
        _raises(lambda: assert_relocations_bound(_FIXTURE_ROWS, (Relocation(dest="nope"),)),
                "name no staged row"),
    )
    _record(
        "binder: a staged row with NO relocation is the normal case, never an error",
        assert_relocations_bound(_FIXTURE_ROWS, ()) is None,
    )
    # The SCOPE paragraph lists the pieces this file deliberately does NOT carry, by box id. That
    # sentence has now gone stale twice - P4.29 landed the lipo merge while it still read as
    # excluded, and P4.30 landed the relocation the same way - and nothing sees it: G8 is
    # diff-scoped so unchanged prose is invisible, G68 is `.md`-only, and plan-lint check 29 scans
    # `.rs` and matches deadness-UNTIL constructions, not exclusion lists. This is check 29's
    # mechanic applied to this file: pull the ids out of that sentence and refuse any that the plan
    # already marks `[x]`. [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]
    # The PROPERTY is "a piece listed as not-carried-here has not since landed HERE" - which is
    # NOT the same as "that box is done somewhere". Keying on the plan's `[x]` would red on any box
    # whose work lands elsewhere, and the only way to green that would be deleting a cross-reference
    # that is still true. So it keys on THIS FILE: a box that landed work here leaves a
    # `[Build-Session-Entscheidung: <id>]` tag at the code site, which is the repo's own marker for
    # exactly that. P4.29, P4.30 and P4.31 all have one; the two still listed have none.
    # (P4.31 was the example this comment once used for "a box that never touches this file" - and
    # it turned out to touch it, because the config's binder has to live in an un-caged file that
    # package time actually reaches. The tag-keyed form was right for a reason that outlived its
    # illustration.) [Build-Session-Entscheidung: P4.30]
    _scope_parts = __doc__.split("Each excluded piece is its own")
    # Guarded, not indexed: a reworded sentinel would otherwise raise IndexError at suite top
    # level and kill every later leg with a traceback - the shape `_raises` just fixed one scope
    # down. An empty sentence reds the non-vacuity leg instead, which is the right signal.
    _scope_sentence = _scope_parts[1].split("The macOS dual-arch")[0] if len(_scope_parts) > 1 else ""
    _excluded_ids = re.findall(r"\(P(\d+\.\d+)\)", _scope_sentence)
    _own_source = Path(__file__).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    _decided_here = set(re.findall(r"Build-Session-Entscheidung: P(\d+\.\d+(?:\.\d+)?)", _own_source))
    _record(
        # RESIDUAL, named: the tag is a heuristic for "landed here". A box that adds code here
        # WITHOUT a self-made choice site leaves no tag, and the sentence would stay silently
        # stale. It is the best signal the repo already keeps; the alternative (plan `[x]`) is
        # strictly worse, since it reds on boxes that never touch this file at all.
        "SCOPE freshness: no piece the docstring lists as EXCLUDED has since landed in THIS file - "
        "the sentence went stale at P4.29 and again at P4.30, and no gate could see it (G8 is "
        "diff-scoped, G68 is .md-only, plan-lint check 29 is .rs-only)",
        scope_ids_still_absent(_excluded_ids, _decided_here),
    )
    _record(
        "SCOPE freshness: the leg is non-vacuous - it finds ids in that sentence AND finds boxes "
        "that DID decide here, so an empty either side cannot pass it silently",
        # Bounded at 1, not at the current 2: the P4.41 forward note schedules REMOVING one of the
        # listed ids, and a leg pinned to today's length would red on that correct future edit
        # (P4.31 has since removed a third the same way). Emptiness is what vacuity means here.
        len(_excluded_ids) >= 1 and len(_decided_here) >= 1,
    )
    _record(
        "SCOPE freshness: the COMPARISON itself is driven, not just its live inputs - a listed id "
        "that DID decide here is refused, one that did not is accepted (no listed id is currently "
        "decided here, so the live evaluation alone cannot tell a working check from a dropped one)",
        scope_ids_still_absent(["4.30"], ["4.27", "4.30"]) is False
        and scope_ids_still_absent(["4.41"], ["4.27", "4.30"]) is True
        and scope_ids_still_absent([], ["4.30"]) is False,
    )
    _record(
        "_raises: the NEEDLE decides, not the type alone - a StagingError whose message does not "
        "contain the needle is False, which is what makes every arm-blind leg in this file "
        "impossible to write (the docstring claims that; this leg is what proves it)",
        _raises(lambda: relocator_for("zzz-unknown-triple"), "a phrase that arm does not use")
        is False,
    )
    _record(
        "_raises: a WRONG exception type is a named FAIL, never a dead suite - it used to "
        "propagate and kill the whole run with a traceback instead of reddening the one leg. "
        "The needle IS present in the KeyError, so only the TYPE can decide this leg",
        _raises(lambda: {}["boom"], "boom") is False,
    )

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as tmp:
        base = Path(tmp).resolve()
        original = b"\xcf\xfa\xed\xfe" + b"mach-o body"

        def _target(name: str) -> Path:
            return _write_bytes(base / name, original)

        happy = _RelocationRunner(readback="@loader_path/lib.dylib\n")
